window.WIKI = {"p":{"p01":{"t":"Between genotype and phenotype lies a whole system of developmental processes, which Waddington named the epigenotype, and studying how genes act through it is 'epigenetics'.","bg":"Classical genetics inferred genotypes by observing adult phenotypes and treated the route from one to the other as a black box that could be ignored for the purposes of studying inheritance. Waddington argued that the black box is the heart of the developmental problem and deserves its own science.","ap":"A short essay that defines terms and illustrates them with developmental examples: single genes with cascading effects in mouse and rat, and the stepwise development of the Drosophila wing.","f":["Describing developmental processes is a first step (Haecker's 'phenogenetics'); the second and more important step is discovering the causal mechanisms behind them, which he proposes to call epigenetics.","Epigenotype is the name for the whole complex of developmental processes connecting genotype and phenotype.","A single mutant gene can set off a long chain of secondary effects (for example the grey-lethal mouse, where failed bone resorption eventually starves the animal, and a rat gene whose effects begin in cartilage and reach lungs and circulation).","The Drosophila wing passes through distinct stages (solid, then inflated and hollow, then contracting, then folded); different mutants disrupt the contraction phase in different ways, producing extra veins, fluid bubbles, or elongated wings.","Genes are not occasional intruders in development; they regulate and guide every phase."],"arg":"Heredity cannot be fully understood by looking only at genes and adults; the developmental system that translates one into the other is what connects genetics to embryology.","st":["Foundational conceptual framing that reappears in every later definition of epigenetics.","Uses concrete developmental examples rather than abstraction."],"lm":["No molecular mechanism; written decades before DNA methylation or histone marks were known.","The modern sense of epigenetics (heritable molecular marks) is narrower and different from Waddington's meaning."],"n":["Original essay 1942 (Endeavour); reprinted in Int J Epidemiol 2012, 4 pages.","Drosophila wing example has four developmental stages."],"terms":["epigenetics-definition","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p02","Traces the later, molecular history of the field Waddington named."],["p17","Shows how the term now spans development, mechanism and inheritance, which they say must be kept distinct."],["p13","Developmental plasticity is the modern evolutionary-developmental version of the genotype-to-phenotype question."],["p60","Same author: how environmentally induced developmental outcomes can become genetically fixed."]],"ex":["How has the meaning of 'epigenetics' changed since Waddington, and why does this matter when evaluating claims?","What does the 'epigenotype' idea imply about why identical genotypes can produce different phenotypes?"]},"p02":{"t":"A decade-by-decade history of DNA methylation from the 1925 discovery of 5-methylcytosine to base-resolution maps, showing which early hypotheses held up and what is still unresolved.","bg":"Methylation was reported in bacteria in 1925, but its biological importance was not obvious for decades. A quarter century after Jaenisch asked why anyone should care about DNA methylation, the authors revisit the question with the full history.","ap":"Feature review organized chronologically (1900s to the present) with boxed key experiments, timelines and a glossary; emphasis on mammals.","f":["Early chemistry: 5-methylcytosine was detected in calf thymus in 1948 and in several species by 1954, and was found only before guanine, showing CpG depletion in the genome.","In the 1970s, methylation in higher organisms was proposed to carry heritable regulatory information; hypothetical 'maintenance' and 'de novo' enzymes and passive loss by replication were conceptual precursors of what was later confirmed.","In the early 1980s, CpG islands were discovered and promoter methylation (but not methylation elsewhere) was shown to inhibit transcription; viral work by Jaenisch and colleagues showed that cloned, unmethylated Moloney leukemia virus copies were active while methylated endogenous copies were silent.","X-inactivation: Riggs proposed in 1975 that methylation might control it, but methylation was ultimately ruled out as the primary initiator, though it contributes to stable silencing on the X.","The 1990s and 2000s elucidated writers, readers and links to histone modifications and 3D genome organization; active demethylation remained unresolved for years.","In the genome-wide era, lowly methylated regions (about 4% of CpGs, 10 to 50% methylated in mouse ES and neural progenitor cells) were found away from CpG islands and are enriched for motifs of cell-type transcription factors."],"arg":"Most core concepts of the field were established early, and many early hypotheses held, but the interpretation of methylation patterns (when it causes versus follows regulation) is still under investigation.","st":["Single best chronological map of the field with key experiments identified.","Open access and cites original experiments, useful for naming primary sources."],"lm":["Mammal-focused; plant and invertebrate methylation get less space.","A historical review, so it does not evaluate recent claims in depth."],"n":["5mC first reported in bacteria in 1925; calf thymus 1948.","LMRs: about 4% of CpGs, 10 to 50% methylation (Schubeler lab data)."],"terms":["dna-methylation","dnmt3","genomic-imprinting","transposable-elements","epigenetics-definition"],"cx":[["p03","Structural/enzymatic detail on the de novo writers that the review describes historically."],["p06","Silencing of transposons and viruses by methylation is a recurring theme in both."],["p04","The histone side of the methylation-histone crosstalk the review mentions."],["p01","The original concept that molecular findings eventually filled in."]],"ex":["Trace how the understanding of DNA methylation's function changed between 1975 and 2020.","Which early hypotheses about methylation survived, and which were overturned (for example, X-inactivation initiation)?"]},"p03":{"t":"DNMT3A and DNMT3B differ in CpG specificity and in how they read the base flanking the target CpG, which explains why they methylate different genomic regions and why DNMT3B mutations cause ICF-syndrome-specific hypomethylation.","bg":"Mammalian methylation patterns are set de novo by DNMT3A and DNMT3B, which are partly redundant and partly distinct, but the molecular basis of their differences was unknown.","ap":"Structures of the catalytic domains in complex with the partner DNMT3L, a 'deep enzymology' screen with DNA substrates carrying a CpG flanked by 10 random nucleotides on each side (hairpin ligation, bisulfite conversion, sequencing), and cellular methylome profiling by enhanced reduced-representation bisulfite sequencing.","f":["Flanking sequence from the -2 to the +3 position strongly affects methylation by both enzymes; across all 4096 NNNCGNNN contexts the enzymes show different preferences and rates differing more than 100-fold.","A hydrogen bond in DNMT3B's catalytic loop reduces its CpG specificity relative to DNMT3A (more CpH methylation).","Differences at the dimer (RD) interface change the conformation of target recognition domain loop residues, including K777, S778 and N779; K777 acts as a sensor of the +1 flanking base.","In cells the enzymes preferentially methylate different satellite repeats (DNMT3B minor satellite, DNMT3A major satellite) and DNMT3A cannot compensate for DNMT3B at SatII repeats, explaining SatII hypomethylation in ICF syndrome with DNMT3B mutations."],"arg":"Substrate recognition is multi-layered (catalytic loop, interface, flanking-base sensing), so redundancy between the two enzymes is partial and developmental roles differ.","st":["Combines structure, biochemistry and cellular data in one study.","Deep enzymology gives quantitative preferences at scale."],"lm":["Catalytic domains studied in vitro; full-length enzyme context, chromatin and cofactors modulate in vivo targeting.","Methylation at CpH sites is less relevant for most cell types."],"n":["4096 NNNCGNNN contexts analyzed; over 100-fold range in methylation rates.","Bisulfite conversion above 99.5% in the screen."],"terms":["dnmt3","dna-methylation"],"cx":[["p02","Places the discovery of de novo methyltransferases in historical context."],["p04","Histone marks also guide where DNMTs act; a complementary mechanism."],["p12","Methylation differences matter physiologically; Gao explains how sequence-specific writers could create them."]],"ex":["Explain how two enzymes that make the same mark can produce different methylation patterns.","How do enzyme sequence preferences relate to disease (ICF syndrome)?"]},"p04":{"t":"A textbook-style overview of histone modifications (methylation, acetylation, phosphorylation), chromatin remodelers and noncoding RNA, with an eye toward cancer drug targets.","bg":"Epigenetics refers to heritable information beyond DNA sequence; histone modifications, DNA methylation and RNA-based mechanisms are its main levers.","ap":"Chapter surveying histone marks site by site (H3K4, H3K9, H3K27, H3K36, H3K79, H4K20), then acetylation and its readers, phosphorylation, four families of ATP-dependent chromatin remodelers, and noncoding RNA.","f":["H3K9me3 is deposited by SUV39H1/2 in heterochromatin and bound by HP1 proteins, which link neighboring nucleosomes; G9a/GLP make H3K9me1/2 in euchromatin.","H3K79 methylation by Dot1/DOT1L lies in the globular core of histone H3, depends on H2B ubiquitination, and is tied to transcription elongation and DNA damage responses.","Acetylation reflects a balance between acetyltransferases and deacetylases; H3K27ac marks active enhancers, and BET-bromodomain inhibitors such as JQ1 have therapeutic potential but also risks for normal cells and cognition.","Remodelers fall into SWI/SNF, ISWI, CHD and INO80 families, each with characteristic subunits.","Mutations in epigenetic regulators drive tumorigenesis, motivating epigenetic drugs and precision medicine."],"arg":"Histone modifications form a combinatorial regulatory code that sets chromatin state; disruption of the code is a driver of cancer.","st":["Compact, organized catalogue of marks, writers, erasers and readers."],"lm":["Introductory and descriptive; little on the experiments behind each claim.","Not the same paper as Zhang & Sirard 2021, which is about inheritance."],"n":["Four remodeler families: SWI/SNF, ISWI, CHD, INO80."],"terms":["histone-modifications","chromatin-remodeling","dna-methylation"],"cx":[["p05","Goes deeper on one mark class (phosphorylation) and its crosstalk."],["p21","Applies H3K4 versus H3K9 methylation balance to transgenerational memory."],["p02","DNA methylation counterpart to this histone overview."]],"ex":["Compare the roles of H3K4me3, H3K9me3 and H3K27ac in gene regulation.","Why are epigenetic regulators attractive drug targets in cancer?"]},"p05":{"t":"Histone phosphorylation is a versatile chromatin signal that organizes DNA repair, mitosis, apoptosis and transcription, and it extensively cross-talks with other histone marks.","bg":"Histone post-translational modifications act as signals and as platforms that recruit or retain chromatin factors; phosphorylation had been best known for DNA damage.","ap":"Review of phosphorylation sites on H2A(X), H2B, H3 and H4, the kinases and phosphatases that control them, and their roles in repair, transcription, condensation and cell death.","f":["Phosphorylated H2A(X) at serine 139 (gamma-H2AX) marks large chromatin domains around double-strand breaks; kinases include Mec1/Tel1 in yeast and ATM, ATR and DNA-PK in humans; its removal involves histone exchange (SWR1 or INO80 remodelers) and phosphatases such as HTP-C/Pph3.","In mitosis H3S10 and H3S28 are phosphorylated by Aurora B and removed by PP1, and the balance between them controls chromosome condensation.","H3S10ph acts as a master switch in cis, inhibiting phosphorylation at nearby T6 and T11 in vitro.","H2BS36 is phosphorylated by AMPK and linked to expression of metabolic-stress genes; H2BS10 phosphorylation promotes apoptotic chromatin condensation and requires prior H2BK11 deacetylation.","Crosstalk of phosphorylation with methylation and acetylation allows sophisticated control of remodeling."],"arg":"Phosphorylation is not a single-purpose mark; it provides fast, reversible signaling that integrates cell-cycle, damage and metabolic cues with chromatin structure.","st":["Synthesizes many sites and organisms; clear examples of crosstalk."],"lm":["Many mechanisms are in yeast or in vitro and open questions remain (for example the significance of H2AZ dynamics at breaks)."],"n":["Writers/erasers named: Aurora B and PP1 (mitosis), AMPK (H2BS36), Ste20/Mst1 (H2BS10)."],"terms":["histone-modifications","chromatin-remodeling"],"cx":[["p04","Broader survey of marks; this paper is the deep dive on phosphorylation."],["p21","Illustrates how crosstalk between marks governs epigenetic memory."]],"ex":["Why do histone marks that are transient (phosphorylation) matter for epigenetics if they are not heritable?","Give an example of crosstalk between two histone modifications and what it controls."]},"p06":{"t":"Transposable elements are targeted by overlapping epigenetic silencing systems, and that silencing has been co-opted for imprinting, X inactivation, chromosome structure and gene regulation; TEs may have been the original target of epigenetic regulation.","bg":"Genome sequencing showed that TEs make up a large share of many genomes, so cells need ways to restrain their expression and mobility.","ap":"Review covering TE classes (retrotransposons and DNA transposons, autonomous and non-autonomous), the silencing machinery (DNA methylation, histone H3K9 methylation, small RNAs, Polycomb), and how TE-derived silencing shapes chromosomes and individual genes.","f":["Several mechanisms overlap to silence TEs: CpG and other DNA methylation, H3K9 methylation and heterochromatin, and RNA-based pathways.","TEs make up much of pericentromeric and telomeric heterochromatin in most genomes, so they contributed to the evolution of linear chromosome structure.","TEs are a common component of classic epigenetic phenomena: genomic imprinting, X inactivation and position effect variegation.","A TE inserted near a gene can place it under epigenetic control, producing variegated or tissue-specific expression.","Silencing machinery has been recruited for host functions, for example in V(D)J recombination."],"arg":"Epigenetic regulation of host genes looks strikingly like TE regulation, which suggests the machinery evolved first to control TEs and was later co-opted.","st":["Unifies phenomena from plants, flies, worms and mammals under one framework."],"lm":["A 2007 review; later work on small-RNA pathways and TE co-option has expanded the picture.","The argument that TEs were the original target is an evolutionary inference."],"n":[],"terms":["transposable-elements","dna-methylation","histone-modifications","genomic-imprinting"],"cx":[["p02","Gives the broader DNA methylation picture that TE silencing sits within."],["p17","Both discuss which epigenetic marks can be inherited and how TEs are controlled across generations."],["p09","The agouti and AxinFu epialleles are TE-driven examples."]],"ex":["Why might TEs have been the original target of epigenetic regulation?","How do TEs link imprinting, X inactivation and variegation?","How does a TE insertion create a metastable epiallele?"]},"p07":{"t":"Housekeeping genes can be identified statistically as genes with very stable expression across conditions (a low Gini coefficient), and this supports the concept while showing that housekeeping status is largely specific to a species and to the dataset used.","bg":"Housekeeping genes are traditionally defined as stably expressed, essential, maintenance-oriented and conserved, and they are used as normalizers in many assays (for example GAPDH), but the definition had rarely been tested.","ap":"Used the Gini coefficient (GC) of expression across human tissue datasets (GTEx, Human Protein Atlas) and cancer cell line panels (NCI-60), then extended to other species, and compared against a published human benchmark housekeeping set.","f":["In human tissue data the lowest-GC genes recovered about 81% of the benchmark housekeeping genes; in cancer cell line data the overlap was about 70%, which was expected because the benchmark came from healthy tissue.","Genes common to all four datasets were enriched for basic maintenance functions such as cell cycle, mRNA stability, protein folding and protein transport; stress and context-specific responses were not enriched.","Housekeeping genes defined in this way show stable expression across samples of the same species and give statistical evidence that the category exists.","GAPDH, a widely used normalizer, showed large differences in Gini values across datasets, which argues for a more objective definition of housekeeping."],"arg":"Housekeeping-ness is real but should be tested for the system being studied rather than assumed, because commonly used normalizers may not be the most stable genes.","st":["Gives a simple quantitative criterion and tests it across datasets and species."],"lm":["Depends on the datasets, so stability may differ for unusual conditions or cell types; other metrics (for example Tau) could also be used."],"n":["Benchmark housekeeping set: 3688 genes; about 81% (tissues) and about 70% (cancer lines) recovered."],"terms":["housekeeping-genes","epigenetics-definition"],"cx":[["p02","Housekeeping genes are often CpG-island promoters with characteristic methylation patterns."],["p27","Epigenetic clocks rely on CpG sites rather than expression normalizers, but both need stable reference points."]],"ex":["What properties define a housekeeping gene and how well do they hold up?","Why does the choice of normalizer matter for gene expression experiments?"]},"p08":{"t":"Genes affecting facial and vocal tract anatomy show extensive differential methylation in modern humans relative to Neanderthals, Denisovans and chimpanzees, suggesting regulatory changes in these genes helped shape distinctively modern human features.","bg":"Changes in regulatory elements are thought to drive phenotypic divergence, but finding the ones behind human-specific traits is hard because ancient regulatory changes leave no direct trace.","ap":"Built and analyzed 63 reconstructed and measured DNA methylation maps (ancient and present-day humans, archaic hominins, and six chimpanzees), detected differentially methylated regions (DMRs) that arose in modern humans, and tested their anatomical effects with many controls.","f":["The analysis identified 588 genes with methylation states unique to modern humans.","Genes associated with face and vocal tract anatomy underwent particularly extensive changes, with hypermethylation in a network including SOX9, ACAN, COL2A1, NFIX and XYLT1.","The DMRs show substantial methylation differences (above 50% between at least one pair of human groups) and span thousands of bases.","Results were checked against over 50 orthogonal tests and controls, including simulated DMRs, which did not show the same enrichment."],"arg":"Methylation can be used as a window onto regulatory evolution in extinct hominins, and the pattern suggests repression of these genes after the split from Neanderthals and Denisovans.","st":["Combines ancient DNA methylation reconstruction with functional annotation and multiple controls."],"lm":["Ancient methylation is reconstructed from deamination patterns, not measured directly.","Links between DMRs and anatomy are associations; effects were not tested experimentally."],"n":["63 maps; 588 genes; DMRs over 50% difference; 6 chimpanzees."],"terms":["human-evolution-methylation","dna-methylation"],"cx":[["p02","Background on methylation as a regulatory mark."],["p29","Another cross-species, evolutionary use of methylation data."]],"ex":["How can methylation maps of extinct hominins be reconstructed?","Why are face and vocal tract genes an interesting finding?"]},"p09":{"t":"The early developmental environment can alter the epigenome, especially at metastable epialleles and imprinted genes, linking prenatal exposures to adult disease risk.","bg":"Gene-environment studies usually focus on SNPs affecting toxicant metabolism, but epigenetic regulation is also environment-sensitive and matters for the fetal basis of adult disease.","ap":"Review of the history of epigenetics, mechanisms (DNA methylation, histone modification), imprinting, reprogramming in mammals, and nutritional and chemical influences on the fetal epigenome.","f":["Epigenetics was introduced by Waddington in the early 1940s; Holliday and Pugh proposed DNA methylation as a mechanism, and imprinting and X inactivation showed the heritable nature of these marks.","Imprinted genes (expressed in a parent-of-origin manner, with Igf2 and Igf2r the first identified in 1991) depend on epigenetic marks, and many have been found in mice and humans.","Metastable epialleles such as agouti viable yellow (Avy) and Axin fused vary in expression between genetically identical individuals because of methylation at an inserted transposon.","Maternal diet (methyl donors) and chemical exposures during development can shift methylation at these loci and alter offspring phenotype and disease risk.","Epigenetic reprogramming in early development and the germline makes this period especially sensitive."],"arg":"Epigenetic marks at metastable epialleles and imprinted genes are biosensors of early-life environment and a plausible mechanism for developmental origins of adult disease.","st":["Clear account of the mouse models and why transposon-linked loci are informative."],"lm":["A 2007 review; human evidence at that time was limited and the mouse examples may not generalize to most loci."],"n":[],"terms":["metastable-epialleles","genomic-imprinting","critical-windows","dohad","dna-methylation"],"cx":[["p01","Source of the term epigenetics, cited here."],["p11","Human evidence of persistent methylation change after prenatal famine."],["p10","The epidemiology that motivated the search for mechanism."]],"ex":["Why are metastable epialleles useful for studying environmental effects?","How can maternal diet change offspring coat color and disease risk in agouti mice?"]},"p10":{"t":"Adult cardiovascular disease and related conditions are linked to growth and nutrition in fetal and infant life, so the womb may be more influential than the adult environment.","bg":"Adult degenerative disease had been explained by genes interacting with adverse adult environments such as diet and lifestyle.","ap":"Short BMJ editorial summarizing geographic and cohort evidence: regional cardiovascular death rates in England and Wales tracked past neonatal mortality, and historical birth records were being traced for follow-up.","f":["Differences in cardiovascular death rates among 212 local authority areas of England and Wales were closely related to past differences in neonatal mortality.","Most neonatal deaths were associated with low birth weight, and rates were high where mothers had poor health, suggesting the intrauterine environment as a cause.","Early feeding programs lipid metabolism in baboons, and infant food affects motor development in preterm babies, supporting the idea of programming.","Other diseases, such as schizophrenia and obstructive lung disease, may also have maternal-environment origins."],"arg":"The old model of adult disease as genes by adult environment should be extended with programming by the environment in fetal and infant life.","st":["Concise statement of the hypothesis that opened the developmental origins field."],"lm":["Ecological and historical correlations, with no mechanism; confounding by later life conditions is possible."],"n":["212 local authority areas."],"terms":["dohad","thrifty-phenotype","critical-windows"],"cx":[["p48","Proposes the thrifty phenotype mechanism for the same observations."],["p11","Later tested a molecular mechanism."],["p13","Develops the evolutionary framing."]],"ex":["What evidence led Barker to emphasize fetal over adult environment?","What are the limits of ecological epidemiology for causal claims?"]},"p11":{"t":"People exposed to famine around conception during the Dutch Hunger Winter had lower methylation of the imprinted IGF2 differentially methylated region six decades later than their unexposed same-sex siblings, the first human evidence that early environment leaves a persistent epigenetic mark.","bg":"Epidemiology linked early adverse conditions to adult disease and epigenetic dysregulation was hypothesized as the mechanism, but human evidence was lacking; the Dutch Hunger Winter of 1944-45 offers a natural experiment with intact records.","ap":"Hunger Winter Families Study: individuals born in famine-exposed cities and followed up, compared with same-sex siblings conceived outside the famine, with methylation of the IGF2 DMR measured in blood; a separate group exposed late in gestation tested timing.","f":["Periconceptionally exposed individuals had about 5.2% lower IGF2 DMR methylation than unexposed siblings (about 0.48 standard deviations of controls).","Within sibships, the exposed individual had the lowest methylation in about 72% (43 of 60) of pairs.","The association was specific to periconceptional exposure, not exposure late in gestation, pointing to the early embryo as a critical window.","The hypomethylation was similar in size to that seen in rat offspring of protein-restricted mothers and may reflect a methyl-donor deficiency."],"arg":"Prenatal nutrition can cause epigenetic changes in humans that persist for life, and timing matters.","st":["Sibling comparison controls for family background; natural experiment with documented exposure."],"lm":["One locus in blood; other stressors (cold, emotional stress) cannot be excluded; no direct link from the methylation difference to disease is shown."],"n":["5.2% lower IGF2 DMR methylation; P = 5.9e-5; 0.48 SD; 43 of 60 sibships."],"terms":["critical-windows","genomic-imprinting","dohad","natural-experiments","dna-methylation"],"cx":[["p10","The epidemiology this study tries to explain mechanistically."],["p09","Animal evidence on environmental sensitivity of the epigenome."],["p01","Exemplifies environment shaping phenotype through development."]],"ex":["Why is the Dutch Hunger Winter a good natural experiment?","Why does the periconceptional timing matter for the interpretation?","What would be needed to show the IGF2 change causes disease?"]},"p12":{"t":"Differences in maternal licking and grooming and arched-back nursing in rats produce stable differences in methylation and histone acetylation at the hippocampal glucocorticoid receptor promoter, setting offspring stress responses for life.","bg":"Maternal care is known to shape offspring stress reactivity, and the mechanism for such lasting effects on gene expression was unknown.","ap":"Compared adult offspring of high and low licking/grooming and arched-back nursing (LG-ABN) mothers using bisulfite mapping of the GR exon 17 promoter, chromatin immunoprecipitation of histone acetylation and NGFI-A binding, cross-fostering, and central infusion of an HDAC inhibitor (trichostatin A).","f":["Offspring of high-LG-ABN mothers had different methylation at specific CpGs in the GR exon 17 promoter, with attention to the region around the NGFI-A binding sequence (lower methylation in the offspring of high-LG-ABN mothers).","The differences emerged over the first week of life, persisted into adulthood and were reversed by cross-fostering, showing the effect depends on the rearing mother, not on genes.","Differences were accompanied by higher histone H3 acetylation, greater NGFI-A binding, higher GR expression and a dampened HPA response to stress.","Infusing the HDAC inhibitor removed group differences in histone acetylation, methylation, NGFI-A binding, GR expression and HPA responses, supporting a causal link."],"arg":"Maternal behavior programs the epigenome of a stress-regulating gene, giving a molecular route for environmental programming across the lifespan.","st":["Combines correlation, cross-fostering and pharmacological manipulation."],"lm":["Rat hippocampus and one promoter; causal order of methylation, acetylation and NGFI-A binding is not fully resolved; cell-type heterogeneity in tissue."],"n":["Group effect on methylation F = 55.9, P < 0.0001; HDAC inhibitor TSA 100 ng/ml."],"terms":["hpa-axis-gr","early-life-adversity","dna-methylation","histone-modifications","critical-windows"],"cx":[["p15","Human analog: stress effects on FKBP5 methylation across generations."],["p16","Glucocorticoid-driven methylation in humans."],["p53","Review of how early adversity shapes the epigenome."]],"ex":["How does cross-fostering show the effect is environmental rather than genetic?","What does the HDAC inhibitor experiment add?","Why is the GR promoter a good model of environmental programming?"]},"p13":{"t":"Developmental origins of health and disease (DOHaD) can be understood as part of developmental plasticity and life history strategy: fetal and infant cues set a phenotype that is adaptive if the later environment matches, and disease risk rises with mismatch.","bg":"Biomedicine had paid little attention to life history theory and evolutionary developmental biology, while observations linked early life to later disease.","ap":"Feature article integrating life history theory, plasticity, predictive adaptive responses and the thrifty phenotype hypothesis with human growth, birth size and infant feeding data.","f":["Developmental plasticity lets genotypes persist through transient environmental change; cues act mostly early in life.","Predictive adaptive responses are adjustments made early whose benefit arrives later, as in the choice of adult phenotype in desert locusts from maternal cues.","Reduced fetal nutrient supply makes the fetus lower its anabolic demand, an immediately advantageous adaptation seen as analogous to growth restriction.","The thrifty phenotype hypothesis (Hales and Barker) contrasts with Neel's thrifty genotype: thrift is induced by early experience rather than selected in ancestors.","In modern humans, prediction can fail because postnatal environments have shifted to energy-dense food and low expenditure, producing mismatch and chronic noncommunicable disease."],"arg":"The risk of later disease scales with the mismatch between the environment anticipated in early life and the one actually encountered.","st":["Provides a unifying evolutionary framework for DOHaD."],"lm":["Predictive adaptive responses are debated; some effects may be constraints rather than adaptations."],"n":[],"terms":["predictive-adaptive-response","developmental-plasticity","dohad","thrifty-phenotype","life-history-theory"],"cx":[["p48","Thrifty phenotype, discussed here."],["p55","Critiques the predictive adaptive response idea."],["p10","Origin of the observations."]],"ex":["Explain the mismatch hypothesis and what it predicts for modern humans.","How does the predictive adaptive response differ from an immediate adaptation?","Thrifty phenotype versus thrifty genotype?"]},"p14":{"t":"In wild baboons, DNA methylation in adulthood carries signatures of early-life adversity that depend on the type of exposure: resource limitation (low-quality habitat, early drought) affects far more CpGs than low maternal rank, and some of those sites have methylation-dependent regulatory activity.","bg":"Early adversity predicts adult outcomes and fitness in many animals, but whether DNA methylation links the two had been poorly tested in natural populations.","ap":"Reduced-representation bisulfite sequencing of blood from 256 wild baboons (295 samples, some longitudinal) at 477,270 CpG sites, modeled with a binomial mixed model against habitat quality at birth, cumulative adversity, age and dominance rank, plus a baboon-specific massively parallel reporter assay and gene expression data.","f":["Relationships between early environment and methylation were heterogeneous: resource-limitation exposures were associated with many more sites than social stressors such as low maternal status.","Sites tied to early resource limitation were enriched in gene bodies and putative enhancers.","Early adversity and adult male dominance rank share a fingerprint, distinct from the much larger effect of age.","A subset of associated windows showed regulatory activity, and for about 88% of early-drought-associated sites in those windows the enhancer activity depended on methylation."],"arg":"Methylation can serve as a persistent biological embedding of some, but not all, kinds of early adversity.","st":["Prospective environmental data in a natural population; functional assays."],"lm":["Blood only; correlational for most sites; the reporter assay tests regulatory potential in vitro, not in vivo effect."],"n":["256 baboons; 477,270 CpGs; 295 samples; 88% methylation-dependent."],"terms":["early-life-adversity","dna-methylation","natural-experiments","social-determinants"],"cx":[["p12","Rat model for the biological embedding hypothesis."],["p15","Human early-adversity epigenetics."],["p11","Human natural experiment."]],"ex":["What does biological embedding mean and how does this study test it?","Why do different adversities leave different methylation signatures?"]},"p15":{"t":"Holocaust survivors and their adult offspring both differ from controls in FKBP5 intron 7 methylation at the same site, but in opposite directions, with parent and offspring levels correlated: an intergenerational, not proven transgenerational, epigenetic association.","bg":"Stress effects on the next generation's epigenome were shown in animals, but human evidence was lacking; FKBP5 is a glucocorticoid-responsive regulator of the stress response.","ap":"Measured FKBP5 CpG methylation near the intron 7 glucocorticoid response elements in Holocaust survivors (n=32), their offspring (n=22) and demographically comparable parent (n=8) and offspring (n=9) controls.","f":["At bin 3/site 6, survivors had about 10% higher methylation than controls (about 56.4 vs 51.3), and the effect held after adjusting for PTSD.","Offspring of survivors had lower methylation at the same site than control offspring.","Parent and offspring methylation levels were significantly correlated.","In offspring, methylation at another region (bin 2) related to childhood abuse in interaction with an FKBP5 risk allele, and methylation across bins related to wake-up cortisol, suggesting functional relevance."],"arg":"Preconception parental trauma is associated with epigenetic alterations in both exposed parents and their children, pointing to a possible route for intergenerational effects of trauma.","st":["First human demonstration of parent-offspring epigenetic association after trauma; controls matched for several covariates."],"lm":["Small samples; blood methylation; cannot separate germline transmission from in-utero effects, postnatal parenting or shared environment; opposite direction in parents and offspring is hard to interpret."],"n":["n = 32 survivors, 22 offspring, 8 and 9 controls; about 10% higher methylation in survivors; p = .046."],"terms":["fkbp5","hpa-axis-gr","inter-vs-transgenerational","early-life-adversity"],"cx":[["p16","Reviews the FKBP5 biology behind the choice of locus."],["p17","Explains why such human findings are intergenerational and not demonstrably transgenerational."],["p12","Animal precedent for epigenetic programming of the stress axis."]],"ex":["Why is this finding intergenerational rather than transgenerational?","What alternative explanations exist besides germline transmission?"]},"p16":{"t":"FKBP5 modulates glucocorticoid receptor signaling in a feedback loop, and stress, genotype and epigenetic state interact to determine how strongly it responds, making it a model for gene-stress-epigenetic regulation and a candidate biomarker and drug target.","bg":"Stress responses and outcomes vary widely between people, and the molecular basis for this variability is a route to individualized prevention and treatment.","ap":"Review of FKBP5 function in GR signaling and other pathways, its genetic variation (gene-stress interactions), epigenetic regulation including intron 7 methylation, and clinical and translational implications.","f":["FKBP5 is a cochaperone that reduces GR sensitivity; glucocorticoids induce FKBP5, forming an ultrashort negative feedback that is disinhibited when FKBP5 is overexpressed.","The rs1360780 risk allele sits in an enhancer near an intron 2 GRE; the rarer T allele facilitates GR-mediated induction of FKBP5.","GR binding to GREs can induce CpG demethylation, so early trauma in risk-allele carriers may lead to demethylation of FKBP5 GREs, further disinhibition and GR resistance.","Lower methylation near the intron 7 GRE in blood is linked to stronger GR-induced FKBP5 transcription and GR resistance, and was seen with childhood but not adult trauma.","Epigenetic marks are tissue-specific, so peripheral markers must be validated against tissues of interest such as brain."],"arg":"Gene-stress-epigenetic interactions at FKBP5 illustrate how timing and type of stress produce lasting differences in stress reactivity.","st":["Integrates genetics, epigenetics, molecular biology and clinical data."],"lm":["Much evidence is from blood; causality in humans is difficult; tissue specificity limits biomarker use."],"n":[],"terms":["fkbp5","hpa-axis-gr","gene-environment-interaction","early-life-adversity"],"cx":[["p15","Applies FKBP5 methylation to intergenerational trauma."],["p69","Neuroscience of the glucocorticoid-FKBP5 loop (plasticity folder)."],["p68","Classic gene-by-environment study of stress sensitivity."]],"ex":["Describe the FKBP5 feedback loop and how a risk allele disrupts it.","Why is childhood timing important for FKBP5 demethylation?"]},"p17":{"t":"Transgenerational epigenetic inheritance is well documented and relatively common in plants and some animals such as nematodes, but is rare in mammals and has little support for environmentally induced effects in humans, partly because of germline and early embryonic reprogramming.","bg":"Epigenetics is increasingly linked with the hope that diet, pollution or emotions might affect descendants, but the evidence needs careful definitions.","ap":"Leading Edge review defining intergenerational versus transgenerational effects, describing reprogramming in the germline and early embryo, chromatin, RNA and structural-templating mechanisms, and confounders such as behavior and the microbiome.","f":["True transgenerational inheritance requires effects in generations never exposed: for males, F2 and later (the exposed F0 and the germline that becomes F1 are exposed), and for pregnant females F3.","In plants, epialleles and paramutation (as in maize) are inherited, and epigenetic inheritance is usually associated with transposable elements, viruses or transgenes, possibly as a by-product of germline defense.","In mice, the Avy epiallele (an IAP retrotransposon upstream of agouti) produces epigenetic mosaics, but germline reprogramming in mammals limits inheritance, so epialleles are extremely rare.","Environmentally induced epigenetic changes are rarely transgenerational and rarely adaptive, even in plants.","Confounding routes include behavioral effects, in-utero exposure, and microbial transmission."],"arg":"Claims of human transgenerational epigenetic inheritance are premature; cultural transmission, not epigenetic, is clearly Lamarckian in humans.","st":["Clear definitions and a skeptical, mechanism-focused treatment."],"lm":["Written in 2014; later work on small RNAs and sperm mechanisms has added to the debate."],"n":[],"terms":["inter-vs-transgenerational","germline-reprogramming","metastable-epialleles","transposable-elements"],"cx":[["p18","Argues sperm RNAs can carry acquired information."],["p20","Worm evidence of several generations of inheritance."],["p06","TE silencing background."]],"ex":["Why is F3, not F2, needed for transgenerational inheritance after a maternal exposure?","Why are epialleles so rare in mammals?"]},"p18":{"t":"Sperm RNAs and RNA modifications are strong candidates for transmitting paternally acquired traits, because they can be altered by the father's environment and shape early embryonic development.","bg":"The inheritance of acquired traits, once considered heretical, is supported by evidence that paternal exposures can be memorized in sperm as epigenetic information.","ap":"Review of sperm small RNAs (miRNAs, tRNA-derived small RNAs, long noncoding RNAs) and RNA modifications, how somatic and environmental signals reach sperm (including epididymal maturation and possibly extracellular vesicles), and how sperm RNAs act in the zygote.","f":["Injecting miRNAs or tsRNAs into normal zygotes can reproduce paternal phenotypes, including stress and metabolic effects.","In one example, injecting miR-19b (elevated in sperm of mice on a high-fat, high-sugar diet) caused obesity and glucose intolerance in offspring, and the phenotype persisted in later generations, though a single-miRNA dose may not be physiological.","tsRNAs, mostly from 5' ends of tRNAs, are enriched in mature sperm.","Sperm RNA content can be changed after testis through epididymal maturation, which may give a pathway for soma-to-germline information flow."],"arg":"RNA-based information in sperm provides a plausible mechanism for paternal effects, shifting the focus from DNA methylation to RNA.","st":["Synthesizes injection, diet and stress paradigms with mechanisms."],"lm":["Injection doses may exceed physiological levels; how RNAs are altered in sperm and how they act in the embryo are still being worked out; most evidence is in rodents."],"n":[],"terms":["sperm-rna","inter-vs-transgenerational","toxicant-inheritance"],"cx":[["p17","Skeptical counterpart emphasizing reprogramming."],["p22","Sperm epimutations after toxicant exposure."],["p23","Further work on transgenerational epimutations."]],"ex":["What evidence supports sperm RNAs as carriers of paternal information?","What are the limits of zygote RNA injection experiments?"]},"p19":{"t":"Inheritance is broader than DNA sequence: epigenetic, ecological, cultural and parental-effect pathways also transmit phenotypic variation, so evolutionary theory and heritability estimates should be extended to an inclusive concept of inheritance.","bg":"Many biologists call for an extended evolutionary synthesis; the modern synthesis reduced inheritance to genes.","ap":"Review of evidence for epigenetic, parental, ecological and cultural inheritance, with methods (animal models with extra variance-covariance matrices, cross-fostering and similar designs) to partition genetic and non-genetic heritability.","f":["Non-genetic inheritance systems interact with genetic ones and affect evolutionary outcomes.","Cultural inheritance can be studied with social learning methods; for example hand-raised New Caledonian crows make tools spontaneously yet also attend to demonstrations, suggesting a genetic component plus learning.","Animal-model approaches with separate genetic and cultural relationship matrices can estimate an inclusive heritability that adds a social component to genetic heritability.","Inclusive inheritance has implications for missing heritability in human complex traits and for major evolutionary transitions."],"arg":"The theory of inheritance itself needs extending to include all non-genetic inheritance that contributes to phenotype transmission.","st":["Offers quantitative tools, not only a conceptual argument."],"lm":["Many non-genetic effects are hard to separate from shared environment, and their evolutionary importance is debated."],"n":[],"terms":["inclusive-inheritance","inter-vs-transgenerational","gene-environment-interaction"],"cx":[["p62","Related argument on the evidence for transgenerational epigenetic inheritance."],["p17","Skeptical evaluation of epigenetic inheritance."],["p63","Extends the argument for non-genetic inheritance."]],"ex":["What is inclusive heritability and how can it be estimated?","How could non-genetic inheritance explain part of missing heritability?"]},"p20":{"t":"In C. elegans, loss of H3K4me3 chromatin modifiers (ASH-2, WDR-5, SET-2) in parents extends lifespan of genetically wild-type descendants for up to three generations, the first example of epigenetic inheritance of longevity.","bg":"Chromatin modifiers regulate lifespan, which raised the question of whether parental chromatin states are incompletely reprogrammed and affect descendants.","ap":"Genetic crosses of wild-type and mutant worms to produce wild-type descendants of deficient parents, lifespan assays across generations, tests of the H3K4me3 demethylase RBR-2 and the germline, and gene-expression analysis.","f":["Deficiency of ASH-2, WDR-5 or SET-2 in the parental generation extended lifespan of descendants through the third generation.","The inheritance depends on the H3K4me3 demethylase RBR-2 and requires a functioning germline in descendants.","The effect is specific to the H3K4me3 methylation complex and accompanied by changes in gene expression."],"arg":"Histone methylation marks can persist through reprogramming, or the complex controls erasure and replenishment, so parental chromatin manipulation changes offspring lifespan.","st":["Controlled genetics isolates the inherited effect from the mutant genotype."],"lm":["Mechanism is unresolved (retained mark, inherited RNA or protein, or altered erasure); effect fades after a few generations; generality to mammals is untested."],"n":["Lifespan effect persists to F3."],"terms":["chromatin-memory-c-elegans","histone-modifications","inter-vs-transgenerational","germline-reprogramming"],"cx":[["p21","Follow-up in worms showing a histone methylation network controls the memory."],["p17","Places worm findings in the context of transgenerational inheritance."],["p18","Mammalian RNA-based alternative."]],"ex":["Why use genetically wild-type descendants of mutant parents?","What are the possible carriers of the inherited information?"]},"p21":{"t":"A network of histone methylation regulators controls the progressive loss of fertility in spr-5 mutant C. elegans, showing that the balance between H3K4 and H3K9 methylation shapes transgenerational epigenetic memory.","bg":"How epigenetic information passes between generations is largely unknown; deleting the H3K4me2 demethylase spr-5 causes H3K4me2 to accumulate and fertility to decline over generations.","ap":"Genetic screen and ChIP-seq in spr-5 mutants, tests of RNAi pathway involvement (rde-1, ergo-1), and analysis of methyltransferases, a demethylase and the H3K9 reader EAP-1.","f":["spr-5 mutants showed generational accumulation of H3K4me2 and progressively reduced fertility.","The main RNAi pathways (rde-1 and ergo-1) were not involved in the sterility.","H3K4me1/me2 methyltransferases (SET-17, SET-30) and the H3K9me3 methyltransferase SET-26, plus the reader EAP-1, regulate the phenotype.","H3K9me3 decreases across generations while H3K4me2 accumulates, and loss of EAP-1 chromatin association may drive the transgenerational infertility."],"arg":"Interplay of euchromatic H3K4 and heterochromatic H3K9 methylation regulates transgenerational effects on fertility.","st":["Moves from phenomenon to a molecular network."],"lm":["Another group reported the fertility defect only at elevated temperature, so the phenotype may depend on conditions; C. elegans-specific."],"n":[],"terms":["chromatin-memory-c-elegans","histone-modifications"],"cx":[["p20","Earlier lifespan work from the same worm field."],["p05","Histone mark crosstalk background."],["p17","Review framing."]],"ex":["How do H3K4 and H3K9 methylation counterbalance each other across generations?"]},"p22":{"t":"Transient fetal exposure of rats to DDT promotes obesity and metabolic disease in the great-grandchildren (F3) through both maternal and paternal germlines, with sperm differentially methylated regions suggesting an epigenetic mechanism.","bg":"Ancestral exposures to toxicants can promote epigenetic transgenerational inheritance of adult-onset disease; DDT's transgenerational effects on obesity were not known.","ap":"Outbred gestating rats (F0) given vehicle or DDT (50 or 25 mg/kg) on days 8-14 of gestation; F1 bred to F2 and F3; pathology, obesity assessment and sperm DMR analysis in F3 males.","f":["Directly exposed F1 animals did not develop obesity but did develop kidney, prostate and ovary disease and tumors.","More than 50% of F3 males and females developed obesity, with testis, ovary and kidney disease linked to metabolic syndrome.","Transmission occurred through both egg and sperm germlines.","F3 sperm carried DDT-induced differentially methylated regions near genes associated with obesity."],"arg":"Ancestral DDT exposure can promote disease transgenerationally, so some obesity etiology may be epigenetic transgenerational inheritance.","st":["F3 is the first unexposed generation after a gestating-female exposure; outbred strain; both lineages."],"lm":["High doses compared with some human exposures; mechanism of inheritance (DMR causality) not proven; controversy about reproducibility."],"n":["DDT 50 and 25 mg/kg; exposure days 8-14; over 50% obesity in F3."],"terms":["toxicant-inheritance","inter-vs-transgenerational","dna-methylation","germline-reprogramming"],"cx":[["p23","Later study of sperm histone retention in the same model."],["p17","Critique of toxicant-induced transgenerational claims."],["p09","Environment and the epigenome in mice."]],"ex":["Why is F3 the transgenerational generation in this design?","What evidence connects DMRs in sperm to the F3 obesity?"]},"p23":{"t":"Vinclozolin and DDT lineage rats show altered sperm histone retention sites in the F3 generation, while a core set of retention sites is conserved and unchanged, supporting histone retention as an additional epigenetic carrier.","bg":"Epigenetic changes in the germline are required to transmit transgenerational phenotypes, and the role of sperm histones had not been tested in these toxicant models.","ap":"ChIP-seq of histone H3 and H3K27me3 in purified cauda epididymal sperm of F3 control, vinclozolin and DDT lineage males (exposure of F0 on days 8-14 of gestation, no sibling or cousin breeding).","f":["A core set of H3 retention sites in control sperm was highly conserved and was not altered by either exposure transgenerationally.","Exposure lineages carried the same core set plus additional differential histone retention sites (DHRs), while change in H3K27me3 was negligible.","The core retention sites overlapped little with those reported in earlier mouse and human studies (only the gene T/BRA overlapped)."],"arg":"Transgenerational changes in sperm histone retention may help mediate epigenetic transgenerational inheritance together with DNA methylation and RNA.","st":["Sperm purified to remove somatic contamination."],"lm":["Descriptive; functional effect of retention changes on embryo and phenotype not shown; labs differ in site identification."],"n":[],"terms":["toxicant-inheritance","histone-modifications","germline-reprogramming","sperm-rna"],"cx":[["p22","Same model and group."],["p18","Alternative RNA-based carriers."],["p17","Critical review of the field."]],"ex":["How might histone retention in sperm affect the embryo?","Why compare F3 and not F1 animals?"]},"p24":{"t":"DNA methylation can be inherited between generations only if it escapes germline and embryonic reprogramming, and the evidence in humans and animals, including dairy cows and possibly mitochondrial DNA methylation, is still limited and debated.","bg":"DNA sequence passes information by replication; in mammals 5-methylcytosine at CpGs is copied at cell division by DNMT1, and the earliest inheritance evidence came from plants (Linaria floral symmetry, Lcyc gene).","ap":"Feature article in Animal Frontiers reviewing mechanisms of 5mC inheritance, paternal and maternal routes, mitochondrial DNA methylation, evidence in different species, and limitations of the field, with emphasis on cattle.","f":["Intergenerational inheritance covers F0 to F1 plus the germline of a fetus (future F2) exposed in utero, so F0, F1 and future F2 share the exposure; transgenerational inheritance requires effects in offspring not directly exposed.","Several prerequisites must be met for methylation inheritance, including that marks persist through reprogramming.","Epididymosomes can transfer small RNAs to maturing sperm, linking soma to germline.","Nuclear and mitochondrial DNA methylation are both treated as possible routes, but mechanisms are still under heated debate.","Limitations include pooled cells masking individual differences, and technical bias."],"arg":"Methylation inheritance is a new and controversial issue that needs more work and validation but could improve genetic evaluation and evolutionary understanding.","st":["Clear definitions of intergenerational versus transgenerational; includes livestock evidence."],"lm":["Review in an animal-science venue; many studies are correlative and species-specific."],"n":[],"terms":["inter-vs-transgenerational","dna-methylation","germline-reprogramming"],"cx":[["p17","More skeptical view of mammalian inheritance."],["p18","RNA route for the same question."],["p22","Rodent transgenerational example."]],"ex":["Define intergenerational and transgenerational inheritance and give the exposure logic for a pregnant female.","What conditions must DNA methylation meet to be inherited?"]},"p25":{"t":"Methylation at a few CpG sites in saliva changes linearly with age, and just two cytosines are enough to predict age with about 5 years of average error, an early proof that methylation can work as an age predictor.","bg":"DNA methylation patterns change with age and contribute to age-related disease; a compact predictor could be useful for biology and forensics.","ap":"Genome-wide screen of 27,578 CpG loci (over 14,000 genes) in saliva from 34 male identical twin pairs aged 21-55, validation of three genes in a replicate sample of 31 males and 29 females aged 18-70, and a two-site regression model.","f":["88 probes (near 80 genes) correlated with age, 69 positively and 19 negatively.","These genes were enriched for age-related disease genes, mainly cardiovascular and neurological.","Methylation at promoters of EDARADD, TOM1L1 and NPTX2 was linear with age over five decades in three sample sets.","A model using two cytosines explained 73% of age variance with an average accuracy of 5.2 years."],"arg":"Age-related methylation changes are regular enough to be turned into a predictor, a precursor to later multi-site clocks.","st":["Twin design reduces genetic confounding; replication in an independent sample."],"lm":["Small samples; one tissue (saliva); only two CpGs, so less accurate than later clocks."],"n":["27,578 CpGs; 88 probes; 80 genes; 73% variance; 5.2 years error."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","dna-methylation"],"cx":[["p26","Scales the idea to hundreds of thousands of CpGs in blood."],["p27","Multi-tissue clock that supersedes it."]],"ex":["Why is a small number of CpGs enough for a first age predictor, and what is lost?"]},"p26":{"t":"A 71-marker blood methylation model predicts age with high accuracy and its deviations capture individual differences in the rate of aging, linked to sex, genetics and tumor tissue.","bg":"Chronological age is linked to methylation, but the methylome had not been used to compare human aging rates.","ap":"Illumina 450K profiling (485,577 CpGs) of whole blood from 656 individuals aged 19-101 in two cohorts (N = 482 and 174, Caucasian and Hispanic), with penalized regression to build the model and validation in other data.","f":["The model used 71 methylation markers; correlation between age and predicted age was 96% with error of 3.9 years.","Markers lie in or near genes tied to Alzheimer's disease, cancer, tissue degradation, DNA damage and oxidative stress (for example SST and KLF14).","The apparent methylomic aging rate varies between individuals and is affected by sex and genetic variants.","Aging rate differences help explain epigenetic drift and are reflected in the transcriptome.","The model holds in other tissues and shows an advanced aging rate in tumor tissue."],"arg":"Methylation profiles give a quantitative readout of aging rate, not just age.","st":["Large age range and two cohorts; tests in other tissues."],"lm":["Blood-specific model; cell composition affects estimates; correlation, not causation."],"n":["656 individuals; ages 19-101; 71 markers; r = 0.96; error 3.9 years; 485,577 CpGs."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","age-acceleration","cell-type-heterogeneity"],"cx":[["p27","Multi-tissue alternative developed at the same time."],["p28","Next generation trained on phenotype instead of age."]],"ex":["What does the apparent methylomic aging rate capture beyond chronological age?"]},"p27":{"t":"A multi-tissue clock of 353 CpG sites estimates DNA methylation age across most tissues and cell types, is near zero in stem cells, and its acceleration is heritable and elevated in cancer.","bg":"It was unknown whether methylation could predict age across many tissues and whether the prediction is biologically meaningful.","ap":"Penalized regression trained on about 8,000 samples from 82 Illumina datasets covering 51 healthy tissues and cell types, with tests in stem cells, cell culture, chimpanzee and about 6,000 cancer samples.","f":["The 353 CpGs form the clock; median test-set error was about 3.6 years.","DNA methylation age is close to zero in embryonic and induced pluripotent stem cells and correlates with cell passage number.","Age acceleration is highly heritable and the clock applies to chimpanzee tissues.","Cancer tissue shows accelerated age, with steroid-receptor mutations in breast cancer strongly accelerating it.","The author proposes methylation age reflects the cumulative effect of an epigenetic maintenance system."],"arg":"A single pan-tissue clock exists, making methylation age a general biomarker for development, cancer and aging.","st":["Broad training set and many validation contexts; freely available predictor."],"lm":["Trained on chronological age, so effect sizes with disease are modest; tissue-specific accuracy varies."],"n":["353 CpGs; 8,000 samples; 82 datasets; 51 tissues; median error 3.6 years; 6,000 cancer samples."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","age-acceleration","cross-species-clocks"],"cx":[["p26","Blood-based counterpart."],["p28","Phenotype-trained successor."],["p29","Mortality-trained successor."]],"ex":["Why does the clock read near zero in stem cells?","What does the median error mean?"]},"p28":{"t":"DNAm PhenoAge, trained on a clinical phenotypic age instead of chronological age, predicts mortality, healthspan and disease risk far better than first-generation clocks.","bg":"First-generation clocks correlate above 0.9 with age but have small to moderate associations with disease, partly because chronological age may exclude CpGs that signal biological age.","ap":"Three steps: (1) phenotypic age from nine clinical biomarkers plus chronological age, trained on NHANES III (9,926 adults, over 23 years of mortality follow-up); (2) regressing it on blood methylation in InCHIANTI to select 513 CpGs; (3) validation across cohorts, tissues and biology.","f":["DNAm PhenoAge outperforms the Hannum and Horvath clocks for all-cause mortality, cancers, healthspan, physical functioning and Alzheimer's disease.","Higher epigenetic age acceleration was associated with about 9% higher all-cause mortality risk (HR about 1.09) and about 10% higher cardiovascular mortality (HR about 1.10), with similar effects for cancer, diabetes and respiratory mortality.","It correlates strongly with age in every tissue and cell tested, although developed in blood.","Higher epigenetic age is linked to more pro-inflammatory and interferon signaling and less DNA damage response, mitochondrial and translation signatures.","Age acceleration is moderately heritable."],"arg":"Training on a biological-aging phenotype rather than calendar age yields a stronger biomarker of morbidity and mortality.","st":["Large validation across outcomes and tissues; link to biology."],"lm":["Still blood-trained; mixing of cell composition; correlation with outcomes does not prove causal aging role."],"n":["513 CpGs; 9 biomarkers plus age; 9,926 adults; HR about 1.09 for all-cause mortality."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","age-acceleration","inflammation-epigenetics"],"cx":[["p27","First-generation baseline."],["p29","GrimAge adds mortality-specific training."],["p30","DunedinPACE measures the pace."]],"ex":["What is a second-generation clock and why does training target matter?"]},"p29":{"t":"DNAm GrimAge combines methylation-based surrogates of seven plasma proteins and smoking pack-years into a lifespan predictor that outperforms earlier epigenetic clocks for time-to-death and disease.","bg":"It was unknown whether plasma protein levels could be estimated from methylation, and whether such surrogates could be consolidated into a powerful lifespan predictor.","ap":"Two-stage approach: (1) build DNAm surrogates of plasma proteins (including PAI-1 and GDF15) and smoking pack-years; (2) combine them with age and sex in a mortality model; validate in large cohorts with CT data and lifestyle factors.","f":["GrimAge predicts time-to-death (Cox P about 2e-75), coronary heart disease (P about 6e-24) and cancer (P about 1e-12) better than other clocks.","Age-adjusted GrimAge (AgeAccelGrim) is associated with comorbidity count and with age at menopause.","DNAm PAI-1 relates to fatty liver and visceral fat (for example correlation about 0.42 with visceral fat volume).","The biomarkers relate to healthy diet and education as expected."],"arg":"Methylation can serve as a proxy for physiology and exposures, and a composite of those proxies is a stronger mortality predictor than a clock trained only on age.","st":["Very large validation; interpretable components."],"lm":["Includes a smoking component, so it partly reflects behavior; blood-based."],"n":["7 plasma-protein surrogates plus pack-years; death P about 2e-75."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","age-acceleration"],"cx":[["p28","PhenoAge, the predecessor."],["p30","DunedinPACE is compared with GrimAge."],["p27","First-generation baseline."]],"ex":["What are the components of GrimAge and why does it predict mortality better?"]},"p30":{"t":"DunedinPACE is a blood methylation test of the pace of aging, built from 20 years of longitudinal decline in organ-system integrity, that predicts morbidity, disability and mortality and shows faster aging after childhood adversity.","bg":"Evaluating geroprotective interventions requires measures that quantify change in the pace of biological aging.","ap":"Used the Dunedin birth cohort (1972-73) with 19 indicators of organ-system integrity measured at four time points over two decades to model pace of aging, distilled by elastic-net regression into a single-time-point methylation score restricted to reliable probes, then tested in five other datasets.","f":["The final algorithm has 173 CpG sites.","Pace of aging in the cohort averaged 1 biological year per chronological year (SD 0.29), ranging from 0.40 to 2.44.","High test-retest reliability, associated with morbidity, disability and mortality, and faster aging in young adults with childhood adversity.","Effect sizes were similar to GrimAge, and DunedinPACE added incremental prediction beyond it."],"arg":"Clocks trained on change over time capture rate, not state, and are suited to evaluating interventions.","st":["Longitudinal training target; reliability filtering of probes."],"lm":["Trained in one birth cohort and validated in a few others; blood-based."],"n":["173 CpGs; 19 indicators; four time points; pace 0.40-2.44."],"terms":["pace-of-aging","epigenetic-clock","age-acceleration","early-life-adversity","clock-generations"],"cx":[["p29","Comparator clock."],["p28","Earlier phenotype-trained clock."],["p14","Early adversity and methylation in baboons."]],"ex":["How does a pace-of-aging clock differ from a clock of biological age?"]},"p31":{"t":"Epigenetic clocks provide DNA methylation biomarkers of aging that fit a unified view linking development, maintenance and ageing, and the review defines what these estimators do and do not measure.","bg":"Clinical biomarkers poorly represent fundamental aging mechanisms, making it hard to test interventions.","ap":"Nature Reviews Genetics review of the DNAm age estimators (Horvath multi-tissue, Hannum blood clock with 71 CpGs, single-tissue estimators, PhenoAge), their associations, and two interpretations of the term clock.","f":["Chronological age is calendar time since birth; biological age is a state-dependent concept.","Estimators are accurate across tissues and over the life course, including prenatal ages, and age acceleration relates to granulocyte counts, BMI, time to death and Alzheimer-related measures in different tissues.","Females have lower age acceleration in blood, and offspring of centenarians also show low acceleration.","The multi-tissue clock suggests an innate epigenetic clock process active in development, differentiation, homeostasis and ageing."],"arg":"DNAm age estimators fulfil the long-sought properties of molecular aging biomarkers and suggest ageing is woven into developmental processes.","st":["Authoritative synthesis from the clock's originators."],"lm":["Written by the clock developers; causal role of clock sites remains unresolved."],"n":["Hannum clock: 71 CpGs; Horvath clock: 353 CpGs."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","age-acceleration"],"cx":[["p27","Source of the multi-tissue clock."],["p26","Blood clock."],["p32","Later review of the field."]],"ex":["Why might methylation age be called a clock?","What is the difference between chronological and biological age?"]},"p32":{"t":"Epigenetic clocks are promising biomarkers and practical tools for risk prediction and for evaluating age-reversing interventions, but the quality of available information limits conclusions.","bg":"Aging involves many changes and DNA methylation patterns have become a measure of biological age.","ap":"Review of classic clocks (Horvath, Hannum, PhenoAge, GrimAge) and their differences, risk prediction for age-related disease, and interventions: cellular reprogramming, lifestyle, and others.","f":["Epigenetic age correlates with morbidity, mortality and other factors, supporting risk prediction.","In a randomized study, improved diet slowed GrimAge and increased physical activity reduced epigenetic mortality risk in cancer-related pathways.","In a trial of 43 healthy men, an eight-week program of diet, supplements, exercise, sleep and stress management decelerated Horvath-clock age.","Even modest lifestyle changes can change epigenetic aging."],"arg":"Clocks are useful both for prediction and for judging whether interventions slow or reverse biological aging.","st":["Broad coverage of intervention studies."],"lm":["Many intervention trials are small and short; clock changes are not proven to equal healthier aging."],"n":["43-man trial; eight-week protocol."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","age-acceleration"],"cx":[["p31","Foundational review."],["p29","GrimAge response to lifestyle."],["p30","Pace of aging tool for intervention trials."]],"ex":["What does it take to show an intervention slows epigenetic aging?"]},"p33":{"t":"Global and site-specific DNA methylation changes accompany aging and can estimate biological age and prognosis; the review treats methylation both as a biomarker of aging and a possible driver.","bg":"Aging brings decreased organ function and increased disease, and epigenetic modifications, especially DNA methylation, are proposed to be key regulators.","ap":"Open-access review of epigenetic modifications in aging (methylation, histone modifications, noncoding RNA), age-associated methylation patterns, clock construction and the relation to disease.","f":["Age is the primary risk factor for cardiovascular disease, cancer, neurodegeneration and diabetes.","Epigenetic changes include DNA methylation, lysine and arginine methylation, lysine acetylation and serine phosphorylation of histones, noncoding RNAs, transcription factor binding and nucleosome positioning.","Regular fluctuations in global DNA methylation allow accurate estimation of biological age and disease prognosis.","The authors conclude methylation serves as both a biomarker of biological age and a driving force of aging, calling for cell-, tissue- and disease-specific models."],"arg":"Methylation changes are central to aging; future work should identify target genes and how stress alters methylation.","st":["Accessible overview combining mechanisms and clocks."],"lm":["Claim that methylation is a driver is more asserted than demonstrated; review-level evidence."],"n":[],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","dna-methylation","histone-modifications"],"cx":[["p31","More authoritative review."],["p32","Another general review."]],"ex":["What is the evidence that methylation drives, rather than reflects, aging?"]},"p34":{"t":"Epigenetic clocks should be judged by how well they predict aging outcomes beyond chronological age, not by how precisely they predict age itself, and the introduction to this special collection summarizes the evidence for first-generation clocks.","bg":"Geroscience holds that targeting aging could delay multiple diseases, but aging lacks an agreed molecular definition, so valid biomarkers are needed.","ap":"Editorial introducing a journal collection testing clocks as biomarkers in population and basic research, including a meta-analysis of 61 studies by Ryan et al.","f":["Since Bocklandt et al. (2011) many age predictors have been built, but the key test is prediction of aging outcomes at the same chronological age.","The meta-analysis of 61 studies found the most robust association of Hannum and Horvath age acceleration with body mass index, and a reliable association with frailty across three studies; other variables were inconclusive.","Most evidence likely comes from Caucasian samples and may not generalize."],"arg":"Utility of clocks rests on validity against outcomes, and a clinical or molecular definition of biological aging is still missing.","st":["Frames the validation question clearly."],"lm":["Short commentary, not a primary analysis."],"n":["61 studies in the meta-analysis."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","age-acceleration","clock-generations"],"cx":[["p25","First clock named here."],["p26","First-generation clock."],["p27","First-generation clock."]],"ex":["Why is predicting chronological age not the best test of a clock?"]},"p35":{"t":"Epigenetic clocks can be built for non-human primates and used to compare aging across species, giving insight into life history evolution and offering tools for demography and forensics.","bg":"Clock-like methylation patterns across the human life span predict chronological age and reflect biological age.","ap":"Review for biological anthropologists explaining how clocks are built (beta values mapped to age with penalized regression) and how comparative primate clocks could be used.","f":["A beta value of 0.40 means 40% of genome copies carry methylation at that CpG; penalized regression maps beta values to age.","Comparative clocks across primates can reveal how aging and life history schedules evolved.","Age estimation from clocks can serve forensic and demographic studies in anthropology."],"arg":"Clocks are a practical comparative and demographic tool in anthropology.","st":["Explains construction clearly for non-specialists."],"lm":["Mostly prospective; limited primate datasets at the time."],"n":[],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","cross-species-clocks","life-history-theory"],"cx":[["p27","Chimpanzee application."],["p36","Human biology applications."]],"ex":["How could primate clocks inform life history evolution?"]},"p36":{"t":"Epigenetic clocks provide tools for human biologists to study reproduction, life history, stress and environment, because deviations between epigenetic and chronological age predict health outcomes better than chronological age alone.","bg":"How fast people age varies and robust tools for biological age were scarce.","ap":"Review of clock construction, applications in human biology (reproduction, life history, stress, environment) and methods guidance on design, sampling and processing.","f":["Clocks use predictable epigenome changes, usually DNA methylation, to estimate age with high accuracy.","Deviations between epigenetic and chronological age predict many health outcomes and mortality risks.","Applications of interest to human biologists include pregnancy and reproduction, life history trade-offs, stress and environmental exposures.","Guidance covers study design, sample collection and technical considerations."],"arg":"Clocks capture fundamental processes tied to biological age and suit studies across the lifespan.","st":["Practical for human biology; covers methods."],"lm":["Review-level; many applications are cross-sectional associations."],"n":[],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","age-acceleration","life-history-theory","social-determinants"],"cx":[["p34","Meta-analysis by the same author team is discussed there."],["p31","Foundational review."]],"ex":["Which human biology questions can epigenetic clocks address?"]},"p37":{"t":"Epigenetic clocks go beyond age estimation to predict mortality and disease risk and to assess interventions, and the review surveys how they are built, what they capture and where they are used.","bg":"Aging is multidimensional and traditional biomarkers capture only limited aspects; DNA methylation shifts with age in a way that works as a biomarker.","ap":"Review in Aging and Disease covering clock generations, biological meaning, applications to disease and lifestyle, and potential for reversing epigenetic alterations.","f":["Clocks derived from methylation estimate biological age and aging rate across tissues with high precision.","They give predictive insight into mortality and age-related disease and separate biological from chronological age.","Reversal of epigenetic alterations is proposed as a route to slow aging and perhaps extend lifespan."],"arg":"Epigenetic clocks use the past recorded in DNA to predict present and future health.","st":["Up-to-date general overview (2025)."],"lm":["Broad review; claims about reversal and lifespan extension go beyond current causal evidence."],"n":[],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","age-acceleration"],"cx":[["p32","Similar scope."],["p39","More technical on methods and causality."]],"ex":["What can epigenetic clocks predict beyond age?"]},"p38":{"t":"A systematic review of 33 phenotypic and epigenetic clocks compares their predictive performance and the statistical or machine learning techniques used for human aging and mortality quantification.","bg":"Aging is the leading driver of disease; biological clocks (phenotypic, using clinical biomarkers, or epigenetic, using methylation) are used to identify interventions.","ap":"Systematic review of proposed clocks, excluding mitotic (cancer risk) clocks and non-human models; reported performance of 33 clocks, modeling methods and the most influential clinical measurements in phenotypic clocks.","f":["Early epigenetic clocks predicted chronological age with correlations of 0.96 or higher.","Epigenetic alterations are one of the nine hallmarks of aging and can be measured by DNA methylation.","The review documents the last decade of clock research and outlines paths for future work."],"arg":"A systematic comparison shows how clocks differ in inputs, methods and performance, which helps choose and improve them.","st":["Systematic approach; covers phenotypic clocks too."],"lm":["Reporting differs across studies so direct performance comparison is limited."],"n":["33 clocks; r 0.96 or higher for early clocks."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations"],"cx":[["p28","A phenotypic-to-epigenetic clock."],["p29","Another covered clock."]],"ex":["How do phenotypic clocks differ from epigenetic clocks?"]},"p39":{"t":"Epigenetic clocks are machine learning tools whose interpretation is limited by statistical versus biological variation, cell-type heterogeneity and unresolved mechanism and causality; single-cell and cell-type-resolved clocks are the emerging solution.","bg":"Clocks estimate chronological and biological age and assess interventions, but computational and statistical challenges limit understanding and application.","ap":"Nature Reviews Genetics review of defining biological age acceleration, statistical paradigms for clock construction, cell-type heterogeneity, mechanisms and causality, with guidelines for rigorous interpretable clocks.","f":["Age acceleration depends on how biological age is defined and on separating statistical from biological variation.","Cell-type composition changes with age and confounds bulk-tissue clocks.","Single-cell and cell-type-specific methods are developing to provide resolution.","Clock mechanisms and causality remain open; clock CpGs may not be causal drivers."],"arg":"Rigorous, interpretable clocks need explicit handling of cell-type heterogeneity and a clearer causal framework.","st":["Authoritative on computational issues."],"lm":["Review; many proposals are not yet tested."],"n":[],"terms":["cell-type-heterogeneity","epigenetic-clock","age-acceleration","clock-generations"],"cx":[["p31","Earlier foundational review."],["p37","Broader review."]],"ex":["Why does cell-type heterogeneity complicate interpretation of clocks?"]},"p40":{"t":"A multi-tissue epigenetic clock for mice shows that the most accurate clock is not necessarily the best at detecting anti-aging effects: ridge-regression clocks detected slowed aging in dwarf mice while others did not.","bg":"Mouse epigenetic clocks can be slowed by gold-standard interventions such as calorie restriction and growth hormone receptor knockout.","ap":"1,189 samples spanning 193,651 CpGs; four clocks built by choosing elastic net or ridge regression and all CpGs or highly conserved CpGs.","f":["Accurate age estimators can be built from highly conserved CpGs.","The most accurate clock came from elastic net regression on all CpGs.","Calorie restriction was detected by all clock types, but only ridge-regression clocks replicated slow epigenetic aging in dwarf mice."],"arg":"There are trade-offs: highly accurate clocks may be suboptimal for detecting beneficial interventions.","st":["Directly compares modeling choices."],"lm":["Mouse-specific; tissue composition effects not eliminated."],"n":["1,189 samples; 193,651 CpGs; 4 clocks."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","cross-species-clocks","clock-generations"],"cx":[["p27","Human multi-tissue counterpart."],["p35","Primates comparative framing."]],"ex":["Why might an accurate age predictor miss intervention effects?"]},"p41":{"t":"In longitudinal data across the life course, epigenetic age acceleration (epigenetic age minus chronological age) declines over time and is only moderately stable, so epigenetic age rises more slowly than chronological age, partly because of survival bias.","bg":"Clocks correlate highly with age cross-sectionally, but little longitudinal data existed on how the gap between epigenetic and chronological age changes.","ap":"Hannum and Horvath clocks applied to repeated measures in ALSPAC offspring (n=986, ages 7-19) and mothers (n=982, 16-60), InCHIANTI (n=460, 21-100), SATSA (n=373, 48-99, 5 waves), Lothian Birth Cohorts 1936 (n=1,054) and 1921 (n=476), using linear mixed models.","f":["The age gap (delta age) showed a declining trend in almost all cohorts for both clocks.","Correlation of delta age across waves ranged from 0.22 to 0.82 for Horvath and 0.25 to 0.71 for Hannum, stronger for samples closer in time.","Epigenetic age increases more slowly than chronological age, especially in the oldest groups."],"arg":"Part of the slowing is survival bias, as healthy people remain in longitudinal studies, and the age distribution of the training sample may also contribute.","st":["Five cohorts covering most of the life course."],"lm":["Observational; cannot separate survival bias from true slowing."],"n":["Cohort sizes 373-1,054; correlations 0.22-0.82 and 0.25-0.71."],"terms":["survival-bias","age-acceleration","epigenetic-clock"],"cx":[["p26","One of the clocks tested."],["p27","One of the clocks tested."],["p30","Addresses pace using longitudinal training."]],"ex":["What is survival bias and how does it affect clock studies?"]},"p42":{"t":"A systematic review of 23 studies (41,607 participants) finds that each 5-year increase in DNA methylation age is associated with an 8 to 15% higher mortality risk, while associations with disease and longevity are inconsistent and possibly affected by publication bias.","bg":"People differ in rate of aging and DNA methylation age may predict disease and mortality.","ap":"PRISMA-based search of MEDLINE, Embase, Cochrane and PsychINFO; bias assessed with Joanna Briggs checklists; two meta-analyses (one per clock) of all-cause mortality.","f":["Twenty-three articles: 4 on ageing and longevity, 11 on age-related disease (cancer, cardiovascular disease, dementia) and 11 on mortality.","Evidence for association with disease risk was present but inconsistent.","Meta-analyses indicated each 5-year increase in methylation age was associated with 8 to 15% increased mortality risk.","Positive publication bias must be considered."],"arg":"Methylation age is associated with mortality, but evidence is too heterogeneous to call it a clinical biomarker for disease or longevity yet.","st":["Formal systematic and meta-analytic methods."],"lm":["Few studies and heterogeneous designs; publication bias."],"n":["23 studies; 41,607 participants; 8-15% per 5 years."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","age-acceleration"],"cx":[["p28","Individual study showing larger mortality effects."],["p43","Parallel review on factors associated with acceleration."]],"ex":["How strong is the evidence that clocks predict mortality, and what are the caveats?"]},"p44":{"t":"In children, epigenetic clocks are mostly associated with health outcomes and exposures, which supports their use to study how early exposures affect biological aging and to monitor preventive interventions, although findings are mixed.","bg":"Use of clocks in pediatric research is rapidly increasing and a review was needed for prevention scientists.","ap":"Systematic review through February 2023 of studies using one of three clocks in childhood, as predictor or outcome; 982 records identified, 908 screened, 68 studies included.","f":["A majority of included studies found significant associations between the clock and the health outcome or between an exposure and the clock.","Findings were somewhat mixed overall.","Authors propose clocks as tools for understanding early-life exposures and for tracking intervention effectiveness."],"arg":"Epigenetic clocks are a useful marker of biological aging pathways in childhood.","st":["Focuses on a neglected age range."],"lm":["Limited to three clocks; adult clocks may behave differently in childhood."],"n":["982 records; 68 studies."],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","early-life-adversity","age-acceleration"],"cx":[["p30","Shows faster pace of aging after childhood adversity."],["p43","Factors associated with acceleration."]],"ex":["What can clocks tell us about children that adult-trained clocks may miss?"]},"p43":{"t":"A systematic review of 156 publications (over 1,300 findings) shows that Horvath, Hannum, Levine and GrimAge clocks tend to agree in direction but differ in magnitude when related to biological, social and environmental factors; BMI, HIV infection and male sex are associated with acceleration.","bg":"Numerous factors have been tested for relation to epigenetic clock acceleration, and general trends needed synthesis.","ap":"Systematic review across four databases including 156 publications, restricted to blood-based clocks, with analysis of 57 relationships measured across four clocks.","f":["Clocks tend to agree in direction of effects but vary in size.","Body mass index, HIV infection and male sex were significantly associated with acceleration of one or more clocks.","Acceleration was significantly related to mortality, cardiovascular disease, cancer and diabetes.","Studies with at least 3 samples measured by the same clock were required for a factor to enter analysis."],"arg":"A numerical and graphical synopsis of a decade of clock research shows consistent associations and gaps for future work.","st":["Large dataset of findings, four widely used clocks."],"lm":["Blood only; many factors had too few studies to analyze."],"n":["156 publications; over 1,300 findings; 57 relationships; 4 clocks."],"terms":["age-acceleration","epigenetic-clock","social-determinants"],"cx":[["p42","Parallel systematic review on mortality."],["p46","Meta-analysis focused on social determinants."]],"ex":["Which factors are most consistently associated with clock acceleration?"]},"p45":{"t":"Methylation-based biomarkers, from epigenetic clocks to blood-based EpiScores, are promising minimally invasive tools for monitoring brain aging and neurological disease risk.","bg":"Brain ageing is monitored with cognitive tests and imaging, but peripheral markers could track it longitudinally with less burden.","ap":"Review of clock construction, first-, second- and third-generation clocks, associations with cognitive function, dementia and lifestyle risk factors, and EpiScores that directly track brain phenotypes and proteomic inflammation markers.","f":["Clocks applied across tissues estimate biological ageing, and EpiScores can track cognition and risk factors such as lifestyle behaviors and inflammatory proteins.","Associations are discussed with cognitive tests, MRI measures and dementia.","Limitations and future directions are outlined."],"arg":"DNA methylation biomarkers can complement cognitive testing and imaging in tracking brain health.","st":["Connects clocks to neurology."],"lm":["Blood markers are indirect for brain; effect sizes modest."],"n":[],"terms":["epigenetic-clock","clock-generations","inflammation-epigenetics"],"cx":[["p29","GrimAge and proteomic surrogates."],["p31","Clock overview."]],"ex":["What is an EpiScore and how does it differ from a clock?"]},"p46":{"t":"A pre-registered meta-analysis of 140 studies (N = 65,919) finds that lower socioeconomic status is associated with faster biological aging, weakly for first-generation clocks (r about -0.03) and more strongly for second- (r about -0.11) and third-generation clocks (r about -0.13).","bg":"Social determinants such as SES, race and ethnicity strongly shape health, but it was unclear which clocks are most sensitive to social inequality.","ap":"Systematic review and meta-analysis (searches February 2024 to September 2025) of 1,065 effect sizes testing SES and race and ethnicity with three generations of clocks, with moderator and publication bias analyses.","f":["Association varied by clock generation (F(2) = 178.10, P < 0.001).","Among individual clocks, GrimAge, DunedinPoAm and DunedinPACE showed the largest effects (r about -0.13 to -0.18).","Sex, tissue and array minimally modified results and publication bias was negligible.","Limitations include inconsistent technical reporting and overrepresentation of high-income countries."],"arg":"Newer clocks, especially those trained on mortality or pace, are more responsive to social inequality.","st":["Pre-registered, large, with sensitivity analyses."],"lm":["Observational; cannot show which exposures mediate; wealthier countries overrepresented."],"n":["140 studies; N = 65,919; 1,065 effect sizes; r -0.03, -0.11, -0.13."],"terms":["social-determinants","clock-generations","age-acceleration","pace-of-aging"],"cx":[["p30","DunedinPACE among top performers."],["p29","GrimAge among top performers."],["p43","Earlier factor review."]],"ex":["Why would newer clocks show larger associations with SES?"]},"p51":{"t":"Economists' natural-experiment evidence supports the fetal origins hypothesis: prenatal shocks, even mild ones such as maternal illness or fasting, affect adult health, education and earnings.","bg":"In the 1950s the fetus was seen as a perfect parasite shielded by the placenta; Barker's work argued that the intrauterine environment, especially nutrition, programs later disease.","ap":"Journal of Economic Perspectives review of the fetal origins literature, with attention to identification (natural experiments, siblings and twins) and to outcomes beyond health, such as earnings.","f":["Birthweight is an imperfect marker of fetal insult: in the Dutch Hunger Winter, exposure early in pregnancy had lasting adult-health effects without altered birthweight, while later exposure cut birthweight but left adults relatively healthier.","Daytime fasting during Ramadan in early pregnancy raised the likelihood of adult disability by over 20 percent among Iraqis and Uganda's Muslims, with larger effects for mental and learning disabilities.","Mild and non-nutritional exposures (infection, pollution, maternal stress) also matter, so effects are not limited to famine.","The review calls the womb a critical period shaping ability and the path of earnings."],"arg":"Early-life conditions have long-run consequences for human capital as well as health, which has policy implications for prenatal care.","st":["Emphasizes causal identification and links economics to biomedical literature."],"lm":["Effects are often estimated on intent-to-treat exposures and mechanism is not identified."],"n":["Ramadan: more than 20 percent increase in adult disability likelihood."],"terms":["dohad","critical-windows","natural-experiments","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p10","The hypothesis being reviewed."],["p11","Molecular follow-up to the Dutch Hunger Winter."],["p52","Biological framing."]],"ex":["Why is birthweight a poor proxy for fetal insult?","What makes a natural experiment useful for fetal origins research?"]},"p55":{"t":"The predictive adaptive response (PAR) hypothesis is a form of developmental plasticity in which early cues set a phenotype suited to the later environment; mismatch is costly, and evidence in humans is contested, so PARs from normative variation should be separated from ill effects of extreme conditions.","bg":"Developmental plasticity is usually beneficial, and the PAR idea has been invoked for disease risk but is controversial.","ap":"Review of the PAR hypothesis, with animal examples, mammalian maternal cues (nutrition, body composition, stress) and a critical appraisal of human evidence.","f":["PARs involve cues in early life shaping a phenotype normally adapted to later conditions; mismatch can reduce fitness and later health.","Cues indicating threatening environments, and variation in nutrition within a normal range, have long-term effects.","In mammals cues come via the mother's plane of nutrition, body composition or stress.","In humans the hypothesis is important to some scientists and controversial to others; distinctions should be drawn between PARs induced by normative variation and ill effects of extreme or pathological conditions."],"arg":"Evidence for true adaptive prediction in humans is weaker than for simple constraint or damage, and tests need to measure fitness-relevant outcomes.","st":["Clear conceptual separation of PAR from related ideas."],"lm":["Humans lack direct fitness tests."],"n":[],"terms":["predictive-adaptive-response","developmental-plasticity","dohad","thrifty-phenotype"],"cx":[["p13","Source of the PAR idea."],["p48","Thrifty phenotype alternative."],["p58","Related plasticity and timescale arguments."]],"ex":["What would count as evidence for a PAR in humans?","How is a PAR different from a constraint or a pathological effect?"]},"p61":{"t":"Genetic accommodation, including Waddington's genetic assimilation, is a plausible evolutionary mechanism supported by Drosophila experiments and a Manduca color polyphenism study, but how often it operates remains to be tested.","bg":"Whether evolution can proceed by genetic assimilation or accommodation is controversial.","ap":"Commentary reviewing experimental evidence, including Drosophila assimilation experiments and a Manduca sexta study showing a color polyphenism evolving by accommodation.","f":["Waddington introduced genetic assimilation in the 1940s and 1950s: a phenotype first induced by an environmental stimulus becomes produced without it.","Several Drosophila experiments suggest assimilation is possible.","A study of the tobacco hornworm Manduca sexta showed a color polyphenism can evolve by genetic accommodation.","Accommodation is the broader process of adjusting the regulation of a novel trait after it first appears."],"arg":"Plasticity can lead evolution by exposing phenotypes to selection before genetic change fixes them.","st":["Concise summary of experimental evidence."],"lm":["Commentary; few systems tested."],"n":[],"terms":["genetic-assimilation","genetic-accommodation","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p60","Original genetic assimilation experiments."],["p65","Updated view of accommodation."],["p62","Related inheritance argument."]],"ex":["Distinguish genetic assimilation from genetic accommodation.","What did the Manduca study show?"]},"p68":{"t":"A functional polymorphism in the serotonin transporter gene (5-HTTLPR) moderates the effect of stressful life events and childhood maltreatment on depression, an influential example of gene-by-environment interaction.","bg":"Stressful life events predict depression, but only some people become depressed; variation in the 5-HTT gene was hypothesized to moderate the response.","ap":"Dunedin birth cohort of 1,037 children assessed from age 3 to 26; 847 Caucasian non-Maori members grouped by genotype (s/s, s/l, l/l) and analyzed for the interaction of genotype and stressful events or maltreatment on depression and suicidality.","f":["People with one or two short alleles showed more depressive symptoms, diagnosable depression and suicidality after stressful life events than l/l homozygotes.","Childhood maltreatment in the first decade predicted adult depression only among s allele carriers (interaction P = 0.05), not l/l.","The genotype itself did not predict depression without stress."],"arg":"Individual response to environmental insult is shaped by genetic makeup, so genotype and environment must be studied together.","st":["Prospective longitudinal cohort with documented stress exposure."],"lm":["Later replications have been mixed; some findings are modest; Caucasian-only subset."],"n":["1,037 cohort; 847 analyzed; maltreatment interaction P = 0.05."],"terms":["gene-environment-interaction","early-life-adversity","resilience"],"cx":[["p69","Molecular and epigenetic analog of the interaction."],["p16","FKBP5 gene-stress interactions."],["p70","Epigenetic route to gene-environment effects."]],"ex":["What is a gene-by-environment interaction, and what is the 5-HTT example?","Why are some replications inconsistent?"]},"p66":{"t":"Methylation differences in Peruvian Quechua relate to developmental and lifelong high-altitude exposure, in hypoxia-pathway and red blood cell genes, and EPAS1 methylation correlates with local genotype.","bg":"High-altitude adaptation is a classic case of natural selection, but the role of epigenetic regulation was not well understood.","ap":"Epigenome-wide methylation study of whole blood from 113 Peruvian Quechua with differing lifetime exposure to altitude (above 2,500 m) using a migrant study design.","f":["Two differentially methylated positions and 62 differentially methylated regions were linked to developmental and lifelong high-altitude exposure.","Hits fell in genes of the hypoxia-inducible factor pathway, red blood cell production and blood pressure.","Some DMPs and DMRs were also associated with fractional exhaled nitric oxide.","EPAS1 methylation was significantly associated with EPAS1 SNP genotypes, so local genetic variation influences methylation."],"arg":"Methylation may mediate both developmental plasticity and long-term adaptation to altitude, while interacting with genetics.","st":["Migrant design separates developmental from lifelong exposure."],"lm":["Blood only; modest sample size; association, not mechanism."],"n":["113 Quechua; 2 DMPs; 62 DMRs."],"terms":["high-altitude-adaptation","developmental-plasticity","dna-methylation","critical-windows"],"cx":[["p67","Evolutionary and physiological view of altitude adaptation."],["p57","Developmental adaptation to altitude."],["p08","Another methylation-and-evolution study."]],"ex":["What does the migrant study design reveal about timing of exposure?","How can genetics and methylation interact at EPAS1?"]},"p50":{"t":"The fetal origins hypothesis is best understood through evolutionary and life history thinking: it raises the tension between pathology, constraint and adaptation, the role of trade-offs, how predictable the environment is, and how energy is mobilized and allocated.","bg":"The Barker hypothesis stimulated epidemiologists and human biologists because fetal growth correlates with chronic disease risk decades later.","ap":"Plenary symposium paper from an evolutionary anthropology perspective, applying life history theory to the fetal origins literature.","f":["Four themes emerge: pathology versus constraint versus adaptation; life history trade-offs; environmental predictability; and energy mobilization and allocation.","Developmental plasticity is a fundamental concept in human biology.","Evolutionary medicine provides a way to interpret early-life effects beyond disease models."],"arg":"Early-life effects should be asked of as possible adaptations with trade-offs and not only as damage.","st":["Clear conceptual framework from a human biologist."],"lm":["Conceptual, with few new data."],"n":[],"terms":["life-history-theory","developmental-plasticity","dohad","predictive-adaptive-response","thrifty-phenotype"],"cx":[["p13","Parallel evolutionary framework."],["p55","Critical appraisal of the adaptation claim."],["p58","Timescale argument for plasticity."]],"ex":["Distinguish pathology, constraint and adaptation in the context of low birthweight.","How does environmental predictability affect whether plasticity is adaptive?"]},"p70":{"t":"The timing of environmental exposure determines how it alters the epigenome: nutrition, behavior, stress, toxins and stochasticity act on the developing epigenome most strongly during early embryogenesis and germ cell development, when marks are being reset.","bg":"Environmental influence on developmental plasticity affects animals from insects to humans, and the epigenetic basis of many altered phenotypes is now understood.","ap":"Review of five environmental factors (nutrition, behavior, stress, toxins, stochasticity) and their action at different developmental windows, with examples such as the agouti (IAP) epiallele, glucocorticoid receptor programming and bisphenol A.","f":["Epigenetic marks are reprogrammed in early embryogenesis and in primordial germ cells, creating windows of heightened sensitivity.","Exposure during these windows can produce lasting changes at metastable epialleles and other loci.","Maternal behavior (GR promoter methylation) and diet/toxicant examples illustrate the range of factors.","Stochastic variation also contributes to epigenetic differences among genetically identical individuals."],"arg":"When an exposure occurs matters as much as what it is.","st":["Integrates windows of vulnerability with several exposure types."],"lm":["Review; animal models dominate."],"n":[],"terms":["critical-windows","metastable-epialleles","dna-methylation","gene-environment-interaction","hpa-axis-gr"],"cx":[["p09","Earlier review with agouti examples."],["p12","GR promoter study cited."],["p11","Human timing evidence."]],"ex":["Why are early embryo and germ cell development windows of sensitivity?","Name five environmental factors that shape the epigenome."]},"p57":{"t":"Developmental adaptation, the idea that conditions during growth shape adult phenotype, explains population differences such as the enlarged residual lung volume of high-altitude Andeans and the metabolic syndrome that follows early undernutrition when adult food is abundant.","bg":"Developmental adaptation is a powerful framework for understanding the origin of population differences in biological traits.","ap":"Feature article reviewing evidence from high-altitude populations and metabolic syndrome research, with support from epigenetic findings.","f":["Individuals who develop at high altitude attain larger residual lung volume, contributing to cardiovascular adaptation in Andean natives.","Development under poor nutrition produces efficient energy use that becomes disadvantageous in environments with abundant food and low energy expenditure.","Epigenetic research in animals and retrospective human work suggest environmental influences on development can persist."],"arg":"Developmental responses, not only genetic differences, shape adult biological outcomes, and mismatch can turn adaptation into risk.","st":["Draws on decades of altitude fieldwork."],"lm":["Distinguishing developmental from genetic adaptation is hard in human populations."],"n":[],"terms":["developmental-plasticity","high-altitude-adaptation","thrifty-phenotype","resilience"],"cx":[["p66","Molecular follow-up on altitude."],["p67","Evolutionary view."],["p13","Mismatch concept."]],"ex":["How does developmental adaptation to altitude differ from genetic adaptation?"]},"p49":{"t":"Events in the earliest stages of human development, even before birth, may influence the risk of diabetes, cardiovascular disease, asthma, cancers, osteoporosis and neuropsychiatric disorders; animal experiments show the insult must occur in a critical period.","bg":"It may seem implausible that a mother's stress, toxins, feeding or infant growth could determine adult chronic disease risk, but evidence is accumulating.","ap":"NEJM perspective summarizing animal experiments and human evidence on developmental origins of health and disease.","f":["More than 40 years ago Widdowson and McCance showed that rat pups undernourished during three weeks of lactation gained weight more slowly for life, whereas a similar deficit at 9 to 12 weeks had only short-term effects, showing a critical period.","Developmental programming of adverse outcomes has been produced in many species by modifying maternal or grandmaternal diet, giving glucocorticoids prenatally, uterine artery ligation, anemia or altering postnatal growth.","Perturbations can cause adverse organ development or adaptive responses that help short term but harm later."],"arg":"Developmental origins of disease should guide prevention and research on early life.","st":["Concise introduction to DOHaD."],"lm":["Short perspective, with broad claims drawn from animal work."],"n":["Three weeks of lactation versus weeks 9 to 12."],"terms":["dohad","critical-windows","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p10","Epidemiologic origin."],["p52","Longer history of the field."],["p51","Economic evidence."]],"ex":["What did the Widdowson and McCance experiment show about critical periods?"]},"p52":{"t":"DOHaD holds that the health and disease risk of later life are affected by environmental factors in preconceptional, prenatal and early postnatal periods, and the field has developed from epidemiology to a conceptual, mechanistic and policy framework including epigenetics.","bg":"The overarching argument of DOHaD is that early environment shapes later disease risk, with emphasis on obesity, type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease.","ap":"Book chapter tracing the origins of the field, conceptual developments and experimental observations, epigenetics, the wider research agenda and public policy.","f":["Focus began with obesity, type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease but extends to endocrine cancers, osteoporosis, frailty, mental health, cognition, respiratory disease and immune function.","Concepts developed include plasticity, predictive adaptive responses and mismatch.","Epigenetic processes are proposed as a mediating mechanism.","The field has implications for public policy on early-life interventions."],"arg":"DOHaD is a broad research and policy concept that bridges epidemiology, evolution and molecular biology.","st":["Authoritative history by field founders."],"lm":["Written by proponents; mechanisms remain partly speculative."],"n":[],"terms":["dohad","developmental-plasticity","predictive-adaptive-response","critical-windows"],"cx":[["p10","Starting point."],["p13","Conceptual root."],["p49","Short earlier perspective."]],"ex":["How has DOHaD changed from epidemiological observation to a conceptual framework?"]},"p47":{"t":"The thrifty phenotype hypothesis proposes that poor fetal and early postnatal nutrition imposes lasting nutritional thrift, including impaired development of the endocrine pancreas, which greatly raises susceptibility to type 2 diabetes.","bg":"Debate over the relative roles of insulin deficiency and insulin resistance in type 2 diabetes, and epidemiological links between low birthweight and later disease.","ap":"Review that outlines the authors' research leading to the hypothesis, reviews literature on fetal and infant growth and diabetes, and reinterprets earlier findings.","f":["In Hertfordshire, where babies born since 1911 were weighed at birth and one year, men with the lowest weight at birth and at one year had the highest death rates from ischaemic heart disease, about threefold higher among 5,654 men.","Poor early nutrition is proposed to impair beta-cell development and cause permanent changes in glucose-insulin metabolism.","The hypothesis reinterprets findings that were hard to explain by genes or adult lifestyle alone."],"arg":"Type 2 diabetes has fetal and infant nutritional origins and is not only a result of adult genetics and lifestyle.","st":["Offers a testable mechanism linked to growth data."],"lm":["Genes versus environment contribution not separated in the original statement; correlation-based."],"n":["5,654 men; around three-fold difference in death rates."],"terms":["thrifty-phenotype","dohad","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p48","Update 9 years later."],["p10","Companion epidemiology."],["p13","Evolutionary reinterpretation (thrifty phenotype versus thrifty genotype)."]],"ex":["State the thrifty phenotype hypothesis and what evidence it was built on."]},"p48":{"t":"Many studies worldwide have confirmed the link between poor fetal and infant growth and later type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome, with a clear link to insulin resistance at all ages, while the roles of genes, maternal hyperglycemia and postnatal growth remain debated.","bg":"The original 1992 hypothesis proposed that poor early nutrition permanently alters glucose-insulin metabolism.","ap":"Review update of epidemiological, clinical and experimental evidence for the hypothesis.","f":["Permanent changes include reduced capacity for insulin secretion and insulin resistance, which combine with obesity, ageing and inactivity in determining type 2 diabetes.","The strength of relationships varies between studies, but the relationship with insulin resistance is clear at all ages studied.","The relationship with insulin secretion is less clear.","The relative contribution of genes and environment remains a matter of debate; maternal hyperglycemia and postnatal growth trajectory need clarification."],"arg":"The thrifty phenotype hypothesis has substantial support but needs clearer separation of genetic, maternal and postnatal contributions.","st":["Reviews a decade of replication."],"lm":["Authors are the proponents; causal mechanism incomplete."],"n":[],"terms":["thrifty-phenotype","dohad","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p47","Original hypothesis."],["p55","Critique of adaptive interpretations."],["p52","DOHaD history."]],"ex":["What has changed in the evidence since 1992?"]},"p62":{"t":"Transgenerational epigenetic inheritance is ubiquitous across taxa, and recognizing it matters for evolutionary theory and practical biology.","bg":"Epigenetics has become a buzz word, and a comprehensive survey of cellular epigenetic inheritance had not been made since Jablonka and Lamb in 1995.","ap":"Comprehensive review of concepts, mechanisms (four types of epigenetic inheritance systems) and a large table of documented cases of transgenerational cellular epigenetic inheritance across taxa.","f":["Analysis of the table shows epigenetic inheritance is ubiquitous.","The review suggests research directions beyond current approaches.","It explores consequences for evolution, including a role for heritable epigenetic variation."],"arg":"Epigenetic inheritance is common enough that evolutionary and practical biology must take it into account.","st":["Systematic table of cases across taxa."],"lm":["Includes many cases of limited generation depth; critics argue mammalian evidence is weaker."],"n":[],"terms":["inclusive-inheritance","inter-vs-transgenerational","epigenetics-definition"],"cx":[["p17","More skeptical treatment."],["p19","Inclusive inheritance."],["p63","Follow-up on germline plasticity."]],"ex":["How common is transgenerational epigenetic inheritance, and what is the debate?"]},"p63":{"t":"Developmental plasticity can involve germline epigenetic changes, and germline epigenetic plasticity increases evolvability by generating heritable, selectable variation including novel functions.","bg":"Plasticity influences the rate and direction of evolution and is partly controlled by epigenetic mechanisms in gene regulation.","ap":"Conceptual and review paper discussing studies in which complex adaptive responses to new challenges are mediated by germline epigenetic processes transmitted over variable numbers of generations.","f":["Plasticity involves not only somatic changes but germline changes.","Heritable epigenetic variation affects both small-scale and large-scale evolution.","Ecological studies and models are reviewed showing how plastic, inherited responses could guide adaptation."],"arg":"A responsive germline links plasticity and heredity and may help populations adapt quickly.","st":["Links plasticity to evolutionary theory."],"lm":["Speculative; relies on cases of limited generation depth."],"n":[],"terms":["inclusive-inheritance","developmental-plasticity","genetic-accommodation","inter-vs-transgenerational"],"cx":[["p62","Survey of cases."],["p60","Genetic assimilation."],["p65","Genomics of plasticity."]],"ex":["How could germline epigenetic plasticity increase evolvability?"]},"p69":{"t":"Stress and trauma lead to DNA methylation changes, in candidate genes and genome-wide, that contribute to stress-related psychiatric disorders such as depression and PTSD, in interaction with genetic predisposition.","bg":"Epigenetic modifications after traumatic experience are emerging as important factors in long-term trajectories toward stress-related disorders.","ap":"Invited review of animal and human studies of methylation in major depressive disorder and PTSD, candidate loci and genome-wide approaches, and molecular mechanisms of how stress can alter methylation.","f":["Childhood abuse leads to lasting demethylation at intronic FKBP5 glucocorticoid response elements in blood and in hippocampal progenitor cells, presumably through GR activation.","Other DNA-binding proteins (estrogen receptor alpha, FOXA1, REST, CTCF) can cause local demethylation near binding sites.","Hydroxymethylcytosine is most abundant in human brain and TET proteins participate in activity-dependent demethylation in neurons; hydroxymethylation accumulates with age in gene bodies.","Examples such as BDNF and Fgf1B promoters show activity-dependent demethylation."],"arg":"Methylation is a mechanism linking stress to lasting vulnerability, and it needs to be integrated with genetics.","st":["Mechanistic and clinical integration."],"lm":["Peripheral tissue may not reflect brain; many findings are correlative."],"n":[],"terms":["fkbp5","hpa-axis-gr","early-life-adversity","dna-methylation","gene-environment-interaction"],"cx":[["p16","FKBP5 review."],["p68","Classic G by E study."],["p12","Rat maternal care model."]],"ex":["How can glucocorticoid receptor activation cause lasting demethylation?"]},"p54":{"t":"Fetal malnutrition and neonatal stress can change the epigenetic state of metabolic and neuronal genes, with possible transmission to the next generation through sperm, and because epigenetic changes are potentially reversible they could allow preemptive medicine for DOHaD-related disorders.","bg":"DOHaD proposes that fetal malnutrition induces thrift, raising the risk of obesity and diabetes in well-fed societies.","ap":"Review in a themed issue on epigenetic mediation of programming effects, covering neurodevelopmental disorders and DOHaD.","f":["Fetal malnutrition alters epigenetic status of metabolic genes and the change can persist, possibly leading to metabolic disorders.","Neonatal mental stress alters epigenetic status of neuronal genes.","Stress-induced epigenetic changes may be transmitted to the next generation via epigenetic status in sperm.","The reversibility of epigenetic changes suggests early detection and epigenetic-restoring drugs could prevent DOHaD-related disorders."],"arg":"Epigenetics links early environment to disease and, because it can be reversed, opens preventive therapy.","st":["Links neurodevelopmental and metabolic programming."],"lm":["Prospective treatment claims are speculative; human evidence is limited."],"n":[],"terms":["dohad","dna-methylation","early-life-adversity","thrifty-phenotype","sperm-rna"],"cx":[["p49","Introduces DOHaD."],["p11","Human epigenetic evidence."],["p18","Sperm routes."]],"ex":["Why does reversibility make epigenetic mechanisms attractive for prevention?"]},"p71":{"t":"Epigenetic mechanisms, especially DNA methylation and histone acetylation, regulate pro-inflammatory molecules (TNF-alpha, interleukins, NF-kB, tumor suppressors and oncogenes), and altered epigenetic landscapes are hallmarks of chronic inflammatory diseases.","bg":"Chronic inflammation drives autoimmune disorders, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, neurodegeneration and cancer.","ap":"Book chapter focusing on pro-inflammatory molecules regulated by enzymes that modify the epigenome.","f":["Pro-inflammatory molecules are constitutively produced in chronic inflammation and are regulated epigenetically.","The most commonly observed changes are aberrant DNA methylation and histone acetylation and deacetylation.","Distinct or global changes in the epigenetic landscape are hallmarks of diseases driven by chronic inflammation."],"arg":"Epigenetic modifiers are therapeutic targets for chronic inflammatory disease.","st":["Links epigenetics to inflammation, relevant to aging."],"lm":["Broad chapter, limited primary data."],"n":[],"terms":["inflammation-epigenetics","dna-methylation","histone-modifications"],"cx":[["p28","PhenoAge clock associated with inflammatory pathways."],["p04","Marks and enzymes background."]],"ex":["How can epigenetic changes sustain chronic inflammation?"]},"p58":{"t":"Human adaptation operates on multiple timescales, and sensitive periods let maternal experience, encoded in her biology and epigenome, inform offspring about gradual environmental change; long-lived species like humans should be buffered against short-term fluctuations.","bg":"Plasticity and critical periods transfer environmental information between generations, helping organisms track slow environmental change.","ap":"Perspective review applying evolutionary and comparative biology to epigenetic processes, DOHaD and transgenerational effects.","f":["Offspring biology responds to experiences encoded in maternal biology and epigenome via the placenta and breast milk.","Transient fluctuations in early experience should have larger long-term effects in small, short-lived species than in large, long-lived species such as humans.","Humans therefore have greater buffering of early-life stress, but also reduced sensitivity to short-term interventions meant to improve long-term outcomes.","Interventions should emulate long-term environmental change to coax the body into a changed long-term strategy."],"arg":"Taking timescales of adaptation seriously improves the design of interventions.","st":["Distinctive argument about timescales."],"lm":["Largely theoretical."],"n":[],"terms":["developmental-plasticity","critical-windows","predictive-adaptive-response","life-history-theory","dohad"],"cx":[["p55","PAR debate."],["p50","Evolutionary framework."],["p13","Life history framing."]],"ex":["Why might humans be buffered against short-term fluctuations in early experience?","What does this imply for short-term interventions?"]},"p65":{"t":"Developmental plasticity can be adaptive and may facilitate adaptation and diversification; genomic studies (transcriptomics and genetic mapping) are identifying environmentally responsive genes and loci that contribute to variation in plasticity.","bg":"The same genotype produces distinct phenotypes depending on developmental conditions, allowing organisms to cope with environmental heterogeneity.","ap":"Review of the evolution of plasticity and its impact on adaptive evolution, emphasizing transcriptomic studies of gene expression across developmental environments and genetic mapping of plasticity loci.","f":["Plasticity can be shaped by natural selection and can facilitate adaptation and promote diversification.","Transcriptomics identifies environmentally responsive genes.","Genetic mapping identifies loci that contribute to variation in plasticity and can fuel its evolution.","Open questions remain about how plasticity genes evolve."],"arg":"Plasticity is genetically variable and thus evolvable.","st":["Up-to-date genomic synthesis across animals."],"lm":["Many examples are from insects and model organisms."],"n":[],"terms":["developmental-plasticity","genetic-accommodation","genetic-assimilation","gene-environment-interaction"],"cx":[["p60","Genetic assimilation."],["p61","Accommodation evidence."],["p63","Epigenetic route."]],"ex":["How can plasticity itself evolve?"]},"p56":{"t":"The model of developmental adaptation combines distal influences (adverse childhood events and paternal care) with proximal ones (current resources and life events) to explain adult adaptation to stress and well-being.","bg":"Theories of stress and adaptation in adulthood focus on recent influences and give too little weight to earlier experience.","ap":"Theoretical model for life-span developmental psychology, linking appraisal and coping to health and well-being.","f":["Adaptation to life stress depends on adverse childhood events and paternal care in childhood as well as internal and external resources available for current events.","Appraisal of past and current events and coping behaviors influence health and well-being.","The model systematically combines biographic distal variables with current proximal influences."],"arg":"Adult development should be explained by integrating personal history and present situation.","st":["Bridges life-course and stress research."],"lm":["Conceptual model without primary data."],"n":[],"terms":["early-life-adversity","resilience","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p59","Resilience framework."],["p68","Empirical moderation of stress effects."]],"ex":["Distinguish proximal and distal influences in the developmental adaptation model."]},"p53":{"t":"Fetal growth and antenatal maternal emotional well-being each predict later psychopathology, but the review finds little support for integrated models in which maternal adversity programs neurodevelopment through the same pathways that control fetal growth.","bg":"The association between birth weight and cardiometabolic health catalyzed the developmental origins hypothesis; the question here is whether the same logic explains mental health.","ap":"Review of literature on fetal growth, antenatal maternal anxiety, depression and stress, and later psychopathology, assessing integrative models of fetal neurodevelopment.","f":["The relation between birth weight and metabolic health is continuous: risk falls with higher birth weight up to macrosomia, where it rises again.","Fetal growth predicts risk of later psychopathology, and antenatal maternal emotional well-being does too.","Integrated models proposing that antenatal maternal adversity acts through fetal growth pathways to program neurodevelopment receive little support."],"arg":"Mental health has fetal origins, but the mechanism is not simply fetal growth restriction, and more specific pathways must be identified.","st":["Careful appraisal of an appealing model."],"lm":["Observational evidence dominates; confounding by genes and postnatal environment."],"n":[],"terms":["dohad","early-life-adversity","hpa-axis-gr","critical-windows"],"cx":[["p12","Animal model for stress programming."],["p10","Cardiometabolic origin of the hypothesis."],["p54","Epigenetics of neurodevelopmental disorders and DOHaD."]],"ex":["Why is the integrated fetal-growth model of mental health weakly supported?"]},"p59":{"t":"Risk and resilience research, viewed through medical anthropology, needs culturally grounded measures of resilience and attention to political economy and life history to explain persistent health disparities.","bg":"Risk and resilience frameworks seek to explain persistent health disparities and translate research into practice and equity-focused policy.","ap":"Annual Review of Anthropology synthesis reviewing cultural narratives, political economy and life history theory, and shortcomings of current work.","f":["Scholars call for sophisticated knowledge of risk, grounded understanding of resilience, and measurement of both across cultures.","Cultural narratives shape what counts as risk and resilience.","Life history theory and political economy offer complementary explanatory frames.","The goal is sustainable health practice and equity-focused policy."],"arg":"Resilience should be understood as a process shaped by social context and not only individual traits.","st":["Integrates anthropology, public health and life history."],"lm":["Review with little quantitative synthesis."],"n":[],"terms":["resilience","life-history-theory","social-determinants","early-life-adversity"],"cx":[["p56","Developmental adaptation model."],["p46","Social inequality and biological aging."],["p14","Adversity and methylation in baboons."]],"ex":["How does a medical anthropology view of resilience differ from a trait view?"]},"p64":{"t":"Plasticity can buy time in a fast-changing environment, because a plastic response can pave the way for permanent genetic adaptation, as shown in lizards, roundworms, yeast and spadefoot toads.","bg":"The classic view holds that evolution results from selection on random genetic changes, but evolution can be too slow in rapidly changing environments.","ap":"Science news feature on research reported at the Second Joint Congress on Evolutionary Biology on links between plasticity and evolution.","f":["Spadefoot toad tadpoles can develop into cannibalistic carnivores with bulging jaw muscles in short-lived pools.","C. elegans normally lays eggs but when food is scarce eggs hatch internally and the young consume their mother.","Evolutionary biologists have shown in multiple organisms that a plastic response can lead to permanent adaptations.","As one researcher put it, the connection is real if you look for it."],"arg":"Plasticity is not a side show but can lead evolution.","st":["Accessible summary of the 'plasticity-first' view."],"lm":["News feature, not a primary source; the plasticity-first view is still debated."],"n":[],"terms":["genetic-accommodation","genetic-assimilation","developmental-plasticity"],"cx":[["p60","Historical root."],["p61","Experimental evidence."],["p65","Genomic detail."]],"ex":["How can a plastic response lead to genetic change?"]},"p67":{"t":"High-altitude vertebrates improve aerobic performance under hypoxia through a mix of plastic and evolved changes along the oxygen transport pathway, and many natives have evolved to blunt maladaptive acclimatization, so evolved effects can be hidden unless animals are reared under common conditions.","bg":"Air-breathing vertebrates need to match tissue oxygen delivery to demand when oxygen availability is reduced at altitude.","ap":"Annual Review of Ecology, Evolution, and Systematics review of cardiorespiratory adjustments, partitioning plastic versus genetic contributions at each step of the oxygen cascade.","f":["Enhanced aerobic performance in natives results from combined environmentally induced and evolved changes at multiple steps.","Many natives attenuate maladaptive acclimatization responses (for example excessive red blood cell production), giving counter-gradient variation.","Where environmental and genetic effects oppose each other, evolved changes may be cryptic in the field and are revealed only by common-garden rearing."],"arg":"Distinguishing plasticity from genetic adaptation requires standardized rearing conditions.","st":["Rigorous integrative framework."],"lm":["Mostly non-human vertebrates, with human evidence summarized in part."],"n":[],"terms":["high-altitude-adaptation","developmental-plasticity","genetic-accommodation"],"cx":[["p66","Methylation in Quechua."],["p57","Developmental adaptation to altitude."],["p61","Related concept."]],"ex":["What is counter-gradient variation and how can it hide evolved changes?"]},"p60":{"t":"Selection for flies that show an environmentally induced trait can make the trait appear without the stimulus, a demonstration of genetic assimilation of an acquired character.","bg":"Under natural selection development becomes canalised so that normal organs form despite environmental variation; Waddington asked whether an environmentally induced variant could be genetically fixed.","ap":"Drosophila melanogaster Edinburgh strain; pupae aged 21 to 23 hours were heat-shocked at 40 C for four hours to induce crossveinless wings (a phenocopy), and lines were selected upward and downward over generations.","f":["At 25 C the strain produced no flies with a broken posterior crossvein, but heat shock produced a number of phenocopies.","Selection for and against the phenocopy produced rapid progress in both directions.","After about 14 generations, some flies in the upward line showed the crossveinless wing without heat shock, and lines were built up that bred true.","The trait has no known adaptive value in nature; the point is whether selection can assimilate a response."],"arg":"Selection acting on the capacity to respond to an environmental stimulus can convert an acquired character into an inherited one without any Lamarckian mechanism.","st":["Classic controlled selection experiment."],"lm":["Selection acts on existing genetic variation in responsiveness, so no mechanism of change is shown; strict virginity of females was not ensured in the breeding."],"n":["40 C for 4 hours; pupae at 21-23 hours; about 14 generations."],"terms":["genetic-assimilation","genetic-accommodation","developmental-plasticity","epigenetics-definition"],"cx":[["p61","Modern evidence for assimilation and accommodation."],["p64","Contemporary examples."],["p01","Waddington's earlier epigenetics paper."]],"ex":["Describe Waddington's crossveinless experiment and what genetic assimilation means.","Why is this not Lamarckian inheritance?"]}},"c":[{"slug":"epigenetics-definition","name":"Epigenetics: what the word means","group":"Foundations","short":"The term has two senses: Waddington's development-focused sense and the later 'heritable change in gene expression without DNA sequence change' sense.","body":"Waddington coined the word in 1942 for the bridge between genotype and phenotype during development. Later the meaning narrowed toward heritability of expression states, driven by work on DNA methylation as a memory mark, imprinting and X inactivation. Today it commonly refers to chemical marks on DNA and histones and to RNA-based regulation that affect expression without changing sequence.","contrast":"Definitions matter for the exam: a mark that is mitotically heritable is not necessarily meiotically heritable, and a transient signal such as a histone phosphorylation may be called epigenetic regulation without being inherited at all.","exam":["How has the definition of epigenetics changed since Waddington?","Why does the lack of a single definition complicate claims about epigenetic inheritance?"],"papers":["p01","p60","p62","p02","p07"]},{"slug":"dna-methylation","name":"DNA methylation","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Addition of a methyl group to cytosine, mostly at CpG dinucleotides in mammals, which usually represses transcription when at promoters.","body":"Methylation is copied at replication by maintenance methyltransferase DNMT1 and set up de novo by DNMT3A and DNMT3B. Promoter methylation is generally linked with silencing, while gene body methylation is linked with active transcription. Methylation patterns vary by tissue, change with age, respond to some environments, and are the basis of epigenetic clocks.","contrast":"Do not confuse methylation level at a CpG (a fraction of cells or alleles methylated) with a causal effect on gene expression: many age or exposure associated sites are correlational.","exam":["Describe how methylation is established and maintained.","What can and cannot be concluded from a methylation difference in blood?"],"papers":["p12","p06","p09","p11","p25","p70","p71","p22","p69","p54","p03","p08","p33","p66","p04","p24","p02","p14"]},{"slug":"dnmt3","name":"DNMT3A and DNMT3B (de novo methylation)","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Enzymes that establish new methylation patterns; they differ in sequence preference and flanking-base reading.","body":"DNMT3A and DNMT3B are partly redundant and partly distinct, and their specificity for flanking sequence shapes where methylation lands. Mattei et al. 2022 uses this to explain why methylation patterns differ between genomic contexts.","contrast":"","exam":["What distinguishes de novo from maintenance methylation?"],"papers":["p03","p02"]},{"slug":"histone-modifications","name":"Histone modifications","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Chemical marks (methylation, acetylation, phosphorylation, ubiquitination) on histone tails and cores that recruit or block chromatin factors.","body":"Acetylation and H3K4 or H3K36 methylation are mostly linked with active chromatin; H3K9me3 and H3K27me3 are linked with repression (heterochromatin and Polycomb). Marks act as signals and as platforms, they cross-talk with each other, and writers, erasers and readers can be drug targets.","contrast":"Phosphorylation is fast and transient (mitosis, DNA repair), while some methylation marks can be more stable; do not assume all marks are heritable.","exam":["Name an activating and a repressive histone mark and the complexes that place them.","Give an example of crosstalk between histone marks."],"papers":["p12","p06","p20","p05","p71","p21","p23","p33","p04"]},{"slug":"chromatin-remodeling","name":"Chromatin remodeling","group":"Mechanisms","short":"ATP-dependent complexes that slide, evict or exchange nucleosomes to change DNA accessibility.","body":"Remodelers (SWI/SNF, ISWI, CHD, INO80 families) act together with histone modifications to open or close chromatin. They help repair DNA, replace histone variants and control gene expression.","contrast":"","exam":["How do remodelers and histone marks work together?"],"papers":["p05","p04"]},{"slug":"transposable-elements","name":"Transposable elements","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Mobile DNA that makes up much of the genome; silenced by methylation, H3K9 methylation and small RNAs, and a source of epialleles.","body":"TEs are held in check by overlapping epigenetic systems. TE silencing has been co-opted for imprinting, X inactivation and chromosome structure, and TE insertions near genes make metastable epialleles such as agouti viable yellow. Some argue the epigenetic machinery evolved first to control TEs.","contrast":"","exam":["How do TEs connect to epigenetic regulation of host genes?","Why are TEs central to epialleles in plants?"],"papers":["p06","p17","p02"]},{"slug":"housekeeping-genes","name":"Housekeeping genes","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Genes stably expressed across tissues and conditions, used as normalizers; their status should be tested, not assumed.","body":"Joshi et al. 2022 used a Gini coefficient to show that stably expressed genes exist, but that commonly used reference genes may not be the most stable ones and that stability is species and dataset dependent.","contrast":"","exam":["What are the traditional criteria for a housekeeping gene?"],"papers":["p07"]},{"slug":"genomic-imprinting","name":"Genomic imprinting","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Parent-of-origin-specific expression of a subset of genes, controlled by epigenetic marks set in the germline.","body":"Imprinted genes such as Igf2 are expressed from one parental allele. Imprints survive the genome-wide reprogramming in the early embryo, so they are a model of epigenetic marks that cross generations, and they are sensitive to the environment during development (for example IGF2 methylation after famine).","contrast":"","exam":["Why are imprinted loci good models for environmental epigenetics?"],"papers":["p06","p09","p11","p02"]},{"slug":"metastable-epialleles","name":"Metastable epialleles","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Loci whose methylation state varies between genetically identical individuals and is set stochastically or by early environment.","body":"The agouti viable yellow and Axin fused alleles are driven by IAP retrotransposons. Methylation at the transposon varies, producing coat color and disease risk differences among isogenic mice, and shifts with maternal methyl-donor supply. They are used as biosensors of early-life exposure.","contrast":"Mammalian epialleles are rare and generally not transmitted far across generations because of germline reprogramming.","exam":["Explain how maternal diet alters the Avy coat color.","Why are metastable epialleles valuable for studying environment effects?"],"papers":["p09","p70","p17"]},{"slug":"critical-windows","name":"Critical or sensitive windows","group":"Early life and development","short":"Periods when the epigenome and physiology are especially sensitive to environmental input, notably periconception, gestation, and early infancy.","body":"Widdowson and McCance showed early undernutrition has permanent effects while a similar deficit later does not. In humans, famine at conception changed IGF2 methylation but later gestational exposure did not (Heijmans). Epigenetic reprogramming in the early embryo and germ cells creates natural windows.","contrast":"A 'window' can be defined by timing of exposure or by tissue development; they are not rigid on-off periods.","exam":["What does the Dutch Hunger Winter show about timing?","Why are germ cell and early embryo development sensitive?"],"papers":["p10","p12","p49","p09","p11","p51","p70","p58","p52","p53","p66"]},{"slug":"dohad","name":"Developmental origins of health and disease (DOHaD)","group":"Early life and development","short":"The idea that fetal, infant and early childhood environment shapes later disease risk.","body":"Started with Barker's epidemiology on birth weight and heart disease, developed through the thrifty phenotype hypothesis, and expanded to a conceptual framework of plasticity, mismatch and epigenetic mediation, covering metabolic, mental, respiratory and other health.","contrast":"Association is not mechanism; economic and epidemiologic natural experiments and animal models give causal support but human mechanism is still thin.","exam":["Summarize the evidence trail from Barker to epigenetic mechanism.","What are the strengths and limits of the evidence in humans?"],"papers":["p10","p47","p48","p50","p49","p09","p13","p11","p51","p58","p55","p54","p52","p53"]},{"slug":"thrifty-phenotype","name":"Thrifty phenotype hypothesis","group":"Early life and development","short":"Poor early nutrition programs lasting thrift (impaired beta-cell development, insulin resistance) that raises type 2 diabetes risk when later food is abundant.","body":"Proposed by Hales and Barker in 1992 and updated in 2001, in contrast to Neel's thrifty genotype hypothesis, which attributed thrift to genes selected in ancestors. The phenotype version places the cause in early experience.","contrast":"","exam":["Contrast thrifty phenotype with thrifty genotype.","What does the thrifty phenotype hypothesis predict about people born small who gain weight?"],"papers":["p10","p47","p48","p50","p13","p57","p55","p54"]},{"slug":"predictive-adaptive-response","name":"Predictive adaptive response (PAR) and mismatch","group":"Early life and development","short":"Early cues adjust development to forecast the adult environment; mismatch between forecast and reality raises disease risk.","body":"Gluckman, Hanson and Beedle framed DOHaD this way. Bateson et al. 2014 argue evidence in humans is weak and that PARs from normal variation should be separated from damage after extreme conditions. Kuzawa and Thayer add that long-lived species are buffered against short-term fluctuations.","contrast":"A PAR needs a fitness benefit in the matched environment; low birth weight with disease can be constraint or pathology rather than adaptation.","exam":["What would count as evidence for a PAR?","Compare predictive adaptation with constraint."],"papers":["p50","p13","p58","p55","p52"]},{"slug":"developmental-plasticity","name":"Developmental plasticity","group":"Early life and development","short":"The capacity of one genotype to produce different phenotypes depending on developmental conditions.","body":"Plasticity helps organisms cope with environmental heterogeneity; it is partly controlled epigenetically and can itself evolve. It underpins DOHaD, polyphenisms (locusts, spadefoot tadpoles, Manduca), altitude development, and can drive evolution through assimilation or accommodation.","contrast":"","exam":["Define developmental plasticity and give three examples across taxa."],"papers":["p01","p60","p47","p48","p56","p50","p49","p61","p13","p57","p51","p58","p63","p55","p52","p64","p65","p67","p66"]},{"slug":"genetic-assimilation","name":"Genetic assimilation","group":"Evolution","short":"Selection fixes a trait that was first induced by environment so it appears without the stimulus.","body":"Waddington's 1953 Drosophila experiment: heat shock produced crossveinless wings; after selection over about 14 generations some flies showed the trait without heat.","contrast":"It does not require Lamarckian inheritance: selection on existing variation in responsiveness can explain it.","exam":["Describe Waddington's experiment and its interpretation."],"papers":["p60","p61","p64","p65"]},{"slug":"genetic-accommodation","name":"Genetic accommodation","group":"Evolution","short":"Broader process where selection adjusts the regulation and expression of a novel, environmentally induced trait.","body":"Braendle and Flatt describe Drosophila and Manduca evidence; Pennisi describes lizards, roundworms and yeast. The 'plasticity-first' view says plastic responses can pave the way for lasting adaptation.","contrast":"Assimilation is the special case where the trait becomes environment-independent.","exam":["Distinguish accommodation from assimilation."],"papers":["p60","p61","p63","p64","p65","p67"]},{"slug":"inter-vs-transgenerational","name":"Intergenerational versus transgenerational inheritance","group":"Inheritance","short":"Intergenerational covers generations directly exposed; transgenerational requires effects in unexposed generations.","body":"For a pregnant female, F0 mother, F1 fetus and F2 germline in the fetus are all exposed, so effects must persist to F3 to be transgenerational. For an exposed male, F0 and the germline that will become F1 are exposed, so F2 is the first test generation.","contrast":"Many human 'transgenerational' claims are intergenerational and cannot rule out shared environment, behavior or in utero exposure.","exam":["Which generation is the first to show true transgenerational inheritance after paternal exposure?","Why is Yehuda et al. intergenerational?"],"papers":["p62","p19","p20","p22","p63","p17","p15","p18","p24"]},{"slug":"germline-reprogramming","name":"Germline and embryonic reprogramming","group":"Inheritance","short":"Waves of methylation erasure in primordial germ cells and the early embryo that limit transmission of acquired marks in mammals.","body":"Heard and Martienssen emphasize that robust reprogramming probably explains why mammalian epialleles are rare, while plants (and worms, to some extent) show frequent epigenetic inheritance. Escapees, imprinted loci and retained histones are the exceptions studied.","contrast":"","exam":["How does reprogramming constrain transgenerational inheritance?"],"papers":["p20","p22","p17","p23","p24"]},{"slug":"sperm-rna","name":"Sperm RNAs and paternal effects","group":"Inheritance","short":"Small RNAs (miRNAs, tsRNAs) in sperm can carry information about paternal environment and affect offspring when injected into zygotes.","body":"Chen, Yan and Duan review injection experiments and routes such as epididymosome transfer. The mechanism is promising but physiological dosing and tissue origin remain questions.","contrast":"","exam":["What evidence supports sperm RNA as a carrier?"],"papers":["p54","p18","p23"]},{"slug":"chromatin-memory-c-elegans","name":"Chromatin memory in C. elegans","group":"Inheritance","short":"Histone methylation states (H3K4me2/3 and H3K9me3) can carry information across several worm generations.","body":"Greer et al. 2011 show lifespan extension inherited for three generations after loss of H3K4me3 modifiers; Greer et al. 2014 show a network of H3K4 and H3K9 methylation regulators controlling progressive loss of fertility in spr-5 mutants.","contrast":"","exam":["What do the worm studies show about histone-based inheritance?"],"papers":["p20","p21"]},{"slug":"toxicant-inheritance","name":"Toxicant-induced transgenerational inheritance","group":"Inheritance","short":"Fetal exposure of rats to DDT or vinclozolin leads to disease in the F3 generation and sperm epimutations.","body":"Skinner et al. 2013 find over 50% obesity in F3 after DDT, and Ben Maamar et al. 2018 find altered sperm histone retention. Proponents see an epigenetic mechanism; skeptics question reproducibility and dosing.","contrast":"","exam":["What evidence links F3 disease to sperm epimutations?","What are the critiques?"],"papers":["p22","p18","p23"]},{"slug":"inclusive-inheritance","name":"Inclusive inheritance and extended synthesis","group":"Inheritance","short":"Genetic plus epigenetic, parental, ecological and cultural channels transmit phenotype variation.","body":"Danchin et al. propose an inclusive heritability that separates genetic and non-genetic components; Jablonka argues for epigenetic inheritance as ubiquitous and for a responsive germline.","contrast":"Humans clearly show cultural (Lamarckian-like) inheritance; biological transgenerational epigenetic inheritance in humans is weakly supported.","exam":["What is inclusive heritability?"],"papers":["p62","p19","p63"]},{"slug":"gene-environment-interaction","name":"Gene-by-environment interaction","group":"Stress and adversity","short":"The effect of an environment depends on genotype, and vice versa.","body":"Caspi 2003 showed that 5-HTTLPR short allele carriers had stronger depressive response to stress. FKBP5 variants interact with childhood trauma to produce lasting demethylation. Later replication of candidate G by E studies has been mixed.","contrast":"","exam":["Give an example of gene-environment interaction and a caveat."],"papers":["p68","p19","p70","p69","p16","p65"]},{"slug":"hpa-axis-gr","name":"HPA axis and glucocorticoid receptor","group":"Stress and adversity","short":"The stress hormone axis; GR in the hippocampus supports negative feedback, and early life can tune its expression.","body":"Weaver et al. 2004: maternal licking and grooming lowers methylation at the GR exon 17 promoter, raising GR expression and dampening stress responses. FKBP5 modulates GR sensitivity.","contrast":"","exam":["Explain how maternal care programs the GR promoter."],"papers":["p12","p70","p69","p15","p16","p53"]},{"slug":"fkbp5","name":"FKBP5","group":"Stress and adversity","short":"A GR cochaperone induced by glucocorticoids, forming a feedback loop; childhood trauma in risk allele carriers demethylates its regulatory elements.","body":"Zannas et al. and Klengel et al. detail the loop, rs1360780 and intron 7 methylation. Yehuda et al. found altered FKBP5 methylation in Holocaust survivors and their offspring.","contrast":"","exam":["Describe the FKBP5 feedback loop."],"papers":["p69","p15","p16"]},{"slug":"early-life-adversity","name":"Early-life adversity","group":"Stress and adversity","short":"Deprivation, abuse, famine or stress in early life that has lasting biological effects.","body":"Evidence runs from rodent maternal care to baboon resource limitation, to human childhood trauma and faster biological aging (DunedinPACE). Effects depend on type of adversity (resource limitation versus social).","contrast":"","exam":["What does the baboon study say about types of adversity?"],"papers":["p56","p68","p12","p69","p59","p54","p15","p16","p53","p30","p44","p14"]},{"slug":"natural-experiments","name":"Natural experiments","group":"Methods","short":"Events outside researcher control, like famines, used to study causal effects.","body":"The Dutch Hunger Winter (records intact), Ramadan fasting in pregnancy and influenza epidemics identify prenatal effects. Designs compare siblings or cohorts to control confounding.","contrast":"","exam":["What makes a good natural experiment?"],"papers":["p11","p51","p14"]},{"slug":"social-determinants","name":"Social determinants of health and biological aging","group":"Stress and adversity","short":"Socioeconomic status and race or ethnicity shape health, and newer clocks show larger associations with disadvantage.","body":"Willems et al. 2026 meta-analysis: first-generation clocks r about -0.03, second -0.11, third -0.13 for SES. Panter-Brick's resilience review frames risk and resilience socially.","contrast":"","exam":["Why do newer clocks track social inequality more?"],"papers":["p59","p36","p43","p14","p46"]},{"slug":"resilience","name":"Resilience","group":"Stress and adversity","short":"Positive adaptation in the face of risk, understood as a process shaped by context.","body":"Panter-Brick 2014 and Martin and Martin 2002 present resilience and developmental adaptation as combining distal history with proximal resources.","contrast":"","exam":["How is resilience defined in medical anthropology?"],"papers":["p56","p68","p57","p59"]},{"slug":"life-history-theory","name":"Life history theory","group":"Evolution","short":"Organisms allocate limited energy among growth, maintenance and reproduction, producing trade-offs.","body":"Ellison and Gluckman et al. use it to interpret early-life effects as strategies; Ryan and Guevara use it for clocks across species.","contrast":"","exam":["Apply life history trade-offs to low birth weight."],"papers":["p50","p13","p58","p59","p35","p36"]},{"slug":"high-altitude-adaptation","name":"High-altitude adaptation","group":"Evolution","short":"Mix of developmental plasticity and genetic adaptation to hypoxia.","body":"Frisancho: developmental adaptation raises lung volume; Childebayeva: methylation in Quechua and EPAS1 link to genotype; Storz and Scott: plasticity and evolved change interact and can be cryptic.","contrast":"","exam":["How can one separate plasticity from genetic adaptation at altitude?"],"papers":["p57","p67","p66"]},{"slug":"human-evolution-methylation","name":"Methylation and human evolution","group":"Evolution","short":"Reconstructed ancient methylomes show regulatory changes in face and vocal tract genes in modern humans.","body":"Gokhman et al. 2020 compare modern humans, Neanderthals, Denisovans and chimpanzees and find 588 genes with modern-human-specific methylation.","contrast":"","exam":["How are ancient methylomes reconstructed?"],"papers":["p08"]},{"slug":"inflammation-epigenetics","name":"Inflammation and epigenetics","group":"Mechanisms","short":"Epigenetic regulation of cytokines and NF-kB links to chronic inflammatory disease and to clock acceleration.","body":"Shanmugam and Sethi review regulation of TNF, interleukins and NF-kB by methylation and acetylation; Levine 2018 links PhenoAge to pro-inflammatory and interferon signaling.","contrast":"","exam":["How does inflammation relate to epigenetic aging?"],"papers":["p71","p28","p45"]},{"slug":"epigenetic-clock","name":"Epigenetic clock","group":"Clocks","short":"A regression model that estimates age from methylation at selected CpG sites.","body":"Built with penalized regression on beta values. Bocklandt (2 CpGs, saliva), Hannum (71 CpGs, blood) and Horvath (353 CpGs, multi-tissue) established the field. Clock age can be reported as predicted age or age acceleration.","contrast":"A clock predicting chronological age very accurately is not necessarily best for disease prediction.","exam":["How is a clock built and validated?"],"papers":["p25","p26","p27","p28","p31","p35","p40","p41","p29","p42","p33","p34","p36","p43","p30","p32","p44","p38","p37","p39","p45"]},{"slug":"clock-generations","name":"Generations of clocks","group":"Clocks","short":"First generation trained on chronological age; second on phenotype or mortality (PhenoAge, GrimAge); third on pace of aging (DunedinPACE).","body":"Newer generations predict morbidity and mortality better and respond more to social factors. Willems et al. and Warner et al. compare them.","contrast":"","exam":["Compare first, second and third generation clocks."],"papers":["p25","p26","p27","p28","p31","p40","p29","p34","p30","p32","p38","p37","p39","p45","p46"]},{"slug":"age-acceleration","name":"Age acceleration","group":"Clocks","short":"The residual of epigenetic age regressed on chronological age; positive means older than expected.","body":"Associated with BMI, male sex, mortality, cardiovascular disease, cancer and diabetes (Oblak) and with 8-15% mortality increase per 5 years (Fransquet).","contrast":"","exam":["Define age acceleration and give correlates."],"papers":["p26","p27","p28","p31","p41","p29","p42","p34","p36","p43","p30","p32","p44","p37","p39","p46"]},{"slug":"pace-of-aging","name":"Pace of aging","group":"Clocks","short":"The rate of biological decline, distilled from longitudinal data (DunedinPACE).","body":"Belsky et al. trained a 173-CpG test on 20 years of change in 19 indicators; average pace is 1 year per year.","contrast":"","exam":["How does pace differ from biological age?"],"papers":["p30","p46"]},{"slug":"cell-type-heterogeneity","name":"Cell-type heterogeneity","group":"Clocks","short":"Bulk tissues are mixtures; changes in composition confound methylation clocks.","body":"Teschendorff and Horvath emphasize single-cell and cell-type clocks as the fix.","contrast":"","exam":["Why does cell composition matter for clocks?"],"papers":["p26","p39"]},{"slug":"survival-bias","name":"Survival bias in longitudinal clock studies","group":"Clocks","short":"Healthy people remain in studies, making age acceleration appear to decline.","body":"Marioni et al. 2018 found delta age declines over time across cohorts.","contrast":"","exam":["How can survival bias affect conclusions?"],"papers":["p41"]},{"slug":"cross-species-clocks","name":"Cross-species clocks","group":"Clocks","short":"Clocks for mice and primates allow comparative and intervention studies.","body":"Thompson et al. 2018 show trade-offs between clock accuracy and sensitivity to calorie restriction or dwarfism; Guevara et al. discuss primate clocks.","contrast":"","exam":["What is the trade-off in mouse clocks?"],"papers":["p27","p35","p40"]}]};
